WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.451 --> 00:00:01.350 line:15%
Hey, everybody.

2
00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.567 line:15%
Welcome or welcome back to "Rollback".

3
00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:08.070 line:15%
"Rollback" is the very special version of the podcast

4
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.920
where we share the best conversations

5
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.250
from our pre-video catalog with you on YouTube

6
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.300
for the very first time.

7
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.300
And today's episode was a bit of a personal thrill for me.

8
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.180
Because despite having a great appreciation

9
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.370
for the benefits of meditation,

10
00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:31.200
consistency and momentum alluded me for many years.

11
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.900
That is, until I discovered Headspace,

12
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.450
which you're probably familiar is a mobile app

13
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.140
prepackaged with an endless rotation of very accessible

14
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.340
guided meditation programs voiced by the company's founder,

15
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.400
and today's guest, Andy Puddicombe.

16
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.730
Originally released in April of 2015,

17
00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.580
this is a thoughtful conversation

18
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.760
that delves deep into the power of meditation practices,

19
00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.170
as well as Andy's quite fantastical personal story.

20
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.020
It's a personal story that involves dropping out

21
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.710
of university to study meditation in the Himalayas.

22
00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.130
Becoming ordained as a Tibetan Buddhist monk,

23
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.060
then training at Moscow State Circus, which is just wild.

24
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.730
And eventually returning to London to draw up the plans

25
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.490
for what would later become Headspace.

26
00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.610 line:15%
And so with that, I sincerely hope you enjoy this exchange

27
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.753 line:15%
with the monk turned mainstream meditation maven,

28
00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:29.532
Andy Puddicombe.

29
00:01:29.532 --> 00:01:32.490
(graphics whooshing)

30
00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.160
In the booth at Headspace HQ.

31
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.569
This is where the magic happens, right? Right here?

32
00:01:37.569 --> 00:01:38.954
That's what I'm told.

33
00:01:38.954 --> 00:01:40.740
I'm a little starstruck.

34
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.548
All of that information coming into my head every morning

35
00:01:44.548 --> 00:01:46.783
happens right in this tiny room.

36
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:48.150
A lot of hours in here.

37
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.000
I know, how many, I mean, how does that work?

38
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.640
Are you in here every day or do you have scheduled time

39
00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.110
once a week where you come in here?

40
00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:55.980
Not at the moment.

41
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.780
So in the past we've done somewhere,

42
00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.192
we reckon it's probably been about 700 hours total

43
00:02:00.192 --> 00:02:02.250
in the studio.

44
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.230
A lot of that was done in the London office actually,

45
00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.540
before we moved out to LA.

46
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.000
But I tend to kind of schedule in a week at a time.

47
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.243
So I'll spend like a week on a pack, on a 30 day pack.

48
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.390
Yeah, and I'm in here most days.

49
00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:19.110
You come in, I mean, are these one takes?

50
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.420
'Cause I'm listening to it and I'm thinking,

51
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.860
how much is this scripted, or is he just so good at this

52
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.440
he just riffs it?
Do you know what?

53
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.490
None of it's scripted.

54
00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:27.323
Really?

55
00:02:27.323 --> 00:02:31.080
So I go in, and I usually try and do

56
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.960
maybe something like kind of five.

57
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.203
Five days, in one go.

58
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.860
I have one line for each day.

59
00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.790
And it's just an idea or a theme for that day

60
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.430
that I've kind of worked out in advance.

61
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:51.000
But other than that, it's not, and it's a funny one.

62
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.730
I actually don't like to do any more than one take.

63
00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.160
So sometimes you'll hear me kind of tripping up

64
00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.320
over the words and we could, if we wanted,

65
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.450
kind of go back and do another take.

66
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.880
But the idea is that it's natural.

67
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.420
And we kind of don't mess around with it once we've done it.

68
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.910
Right, and you just do it, you do it in real time.

69
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.110
Do it in real time.
So you just say it all

70
00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.220
and then space it out

71
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.677
in the timeline?
No, so I sit down,

72
00:03:12.677 --> 00:03:15.300
and as far as I'm concerned,

73
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.983
I'm doing the meditation with you at that time.

74
00:03:17.983 --> 00:03:20.220
I love it, that's pretty cool.

75
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.440
And I infer from social media,

76
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.490
Did you just get back from Necker Island?

77
00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:25.350
(Andy laughs)

78
00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.905
What's going...

79
00:03:26.905 --> 00:03:28.050
I did, I did.
Hang out with

80
00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.970
Sir Richard Branson, what's happening?

81
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.770
I took one for the team.

82
00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:32.880
No one else wanted to go.

83
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.050
Right.

84
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.850
Yeah, I got invited down there

85
00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.700
to do a bit of work over the weekend,

86
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.730
to lead a few meditations and that kind of thing.

87
00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.195
And got back, was it yesterday, I think?

88
00:03:45.195 --> 00:03:46.028
It was pretty amazing.

89
00:03:46.028 --> 00:03:46.959
Yeah.

90
00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.480
I mean, you were only there for like a couple days,

91
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.650
based on Instagram.

92
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.738
From what I can infer.
(Andy laughs)

93
00:03:51.738 --> 00:03:54.480
My forensic analysis of your photos.

94
00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.672
I could only post so many tropical island sunsets

95
00:03:56.672 --> 00:04:01.590
in 48 hours, no, I was there for, yeah, for two days,

96
00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.020
I think it was six plane journeys

97
00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.990
and something like two, four boats in five days.

98
00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.574
It was a long way to go for a short amount of time.

99
00:04:09.574 --> 00:04:11.490
To lead some meditation for it.

100
00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.663
But amazing.

101
00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:17.312
The interesting thing in asking people to meditate there.

102
00:04:18.525 --> 00:04:21.360
It felt criminal to ask them to even close their eyes.

103
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.130
You look out over the ocean and nature does it for you.

104
00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:26.963
It's an amazing thing.
Yeah.

105
00:04:26.963 --> 00:04:29.340
If you can't get some head space there,

106
00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.900
where are you gonna get it, right?

107
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.407
Exactly.

108
00:04:32.407 --> 00:04:33.240
Well, you're a,

109
00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:34.680
I'm looking around your office space,

110
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.790
you have all of these young, energetic people working here

111
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.890
and you're expanding, they're doing construction.

112
00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:44.100
And there's some magic happening here.

113
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.140
And what you've built is really powerful,

114
00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.700
and quite amazing.

115
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.220
But your story is so fascinating

116
00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.010
because you're a most unlikely entrepreneur.

117
00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.590
I am, it's a complete accident.

118
00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.330
And I have to say it is a massive team,

119
00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.600
it's really important to say it's a massive team effort.

120
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.860
I'm essentially the performing monkey in this circus.

121
00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.260
And everyone else is working extremely hard

122
00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.080
and I just get to go out and talk about it.

123
00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.000
Well, you're sort of the axle

124
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.090
around which all of these spokes emanate,

125
00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:15.923
I would imagine.
That's another

126
00:05:15.923 --> 00:05:16.756
way of looking.

127
00:05:16.756 --> 00:05:18.370
But I kind of look at you.

128
00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:21.930
And I was trying to think as I was driving over here,

129
00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.550
like an appropriate analogy,

130
00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:27.000
but it's very much like this sort of postmodern

131
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.610
Siddhartha story or this kind of

132
00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.928
modern take on autobiography of a yogi,

133
00:05:31.928 --> 00:05:35.460
where you're taking these principles

134
00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.520
that date back millennia and trying to find a way

135
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.563
to translate them to a modern audience

136
00:05:40.563 --> 00:05:44.010
so that they can not only understand what you're saying,

137
00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.230
but actually implement them into their lives

138
00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.130
to create sustainable long term positive change.

139
00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:54.339
I think that for me is the most important thing.

140
00:05:54.339 --> 00:05:57.960
There's a whole world out there of books,

141
00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.950
and in back in the eighties of CDs and now kind of podcasts

142
00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.070
and talking about this stuff.

143
00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.550
Discussing it, and there's a lot of thinking about it.

144
00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.680
But there's very little kind of application of it.

145
00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.083
And for me with meditation, more than anything.

146
00:06:15.772 --> 00:06:17.414
It is only in the experience

147
00:06:17.414 --> 00:06:19.740
that we get to see the benefits.

148
00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.220
So finding a way to,

149
00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.080
I see it in three separate kind of camps.

150
00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:26.820
I see kind of awareness.

151
00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.220
So, understanding why we need to do it,

152
00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:30.630
the importance of it.

153
00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.120
Providing a really compelling invitation to try it,

154
00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.880
'cause until we try it, we don't know it.

155
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.470
And then engagement.

156
00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:40.170
How do we keep coming back day after day

157
00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.580
for the rest of our lives?

158
00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.400
And it's only with kind of consistent practice again

159
00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.950
that we kind of really deepen the practice.

160
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.530
So at Headspace, the idea is to yeah,

161
00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.720
try and create some awareness and education

162
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.360
around the importance of looking after the mind.

163
00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:55.860
To get people to come try it,

164
00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.870
and then to encourage people to keep trying it

165
00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.130
on a daily basis.

166
00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:00.930
So there's this gap, however,

167
00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.320
between the education, the knowledge, the information,

168
00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.610
and even the encouragement, and traversing that gap

169
00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:12.480
into actually implementation and action.

170
00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.100
And that's kind of where the magic starts.

171
00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.340
So you can get up and try to talk about how important

172
00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.040
this is to large groups, small groups,

173
00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.643
one on one for the rest of your life.

174
00:07:21.643 --> 00:07:22.476
Yeah.

175
00:07:22.476 --> 00:07:24.270
But how much power do you really have

176
00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.500
over whether somebody's gonna take what,

177
00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.890
even if they walk away from that conversation

178
00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.622
intrigued and interested,

179
00:07:30.622 --> 00:07:33.720
what is the engagement rate with them actually doing it?

180
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.030
And I think what you're working on here

181
00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:38.310
and what I think is really starting to click in

182
00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:39.990
is you really are bridging that gap.

183
00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.250
For some reason, maybe it's deep rooted in something

184
00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.729
you're triggering in the human psyche is working.

185
00:07:47.729 --> 00:07:50.160
Like I, just on a personal level,

186
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.680
I've been playing around with meditation for years,

187
00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.710
the better part of 16 years trying this, trying that,

188
00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.900
this technique, that technique.

189
00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.610
And I have fits and starts,

190
00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.370
and I could never create any kind of consistent momentum.

191
00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.850
And I started using Headspace not that long ago.

192
00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.067
60, 70 days ago or something like that.

193
00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:10.860
And I've really found myself doing it every day.

194
00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.060
I don't know why it's making me do it,

195
00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.190
but I will tell you, I was gonna show you on my phone.

196
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:20.340
Like I have, one thing I did do though,

197
00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.010
that I found effective is I put Headspace

198
00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:24.507
down in the dock.
Okay.

199
00:08:24.507 --> 00:08:26.700
So I can't escape looking at it

200
00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.710
every time I look at my phone.

201
00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.990
That's a good idea. I'd suggest that.

202
00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.220
So I guess.

203
00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.630
I don't even know if I asked you a question

204
00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.400
but I guess the question is,

205
00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:38.580
how do you break down that gap between information

206
00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:40.110
and action and try to bridge that,

207
00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.289
and what is Headspace doing differently

208
00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:45.720
than maybe your predecessors have?

209
00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.540
Yeah. That's a good question.

210
00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:51.732
There's part of me that says I haven't got a clue.

211
00:08:51.732 --> 00:08:54.240
And then there are things.

212
00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.120
So the first thing I'd say is authenticity.

213
00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:00.630
So this isn't that Rich and I kind of went away

214
00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:03.810
on a weekend meditation retreat and kind of came out

215
00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:06.000
and thought, hey, let's set up a company.

216
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.558
So this was as a result of going away

217
00:09:08.558 --> 00:09:10.260
for a very long period of time

218
00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.900
and doing nothing but studying meditation.

219
00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:17.580
And I'm a big believer in lineage and tradition.

220
00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:19.740
Doesn't matter whether it's meditation or if it's in

221
00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:22.740
learning to play the piano or surfing, whatever it is.

222
00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:25.740
If something gets passed down in a very personal way,

223
00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:29.040
over, nevermind decades, but if we start talking about

224
00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.170
hundreds of years or even millennia,

225
00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.643
there's something really,

226
00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.240
is like a refinement and development that takes place,

227
00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.062
which is really powerful, I think.

228
00:09:39.062 --> 00:09:41.517
So I learned from my teacher who learned from their teacher

229
00:09:41.517 --> 00:09:43.650
and that goes back a long, long way.

230
00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.140
And I think a big part of this

231
00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.730
is a very kind of gradual approach.

232
00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:51.217
When you come to the app, you learn,

233
00:09:51.217 --> 00:09:52.860
and you come back the next day

234
00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.300
and you're building on the day before.

235
00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:58.200
So it's this step by step learning kind of process.

236
00:09:58.200 --> 00:09:59.790
I think there's something very valuable in that.

237
00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.770
In the past, back in the eighties,

238
00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.990
you buy a meditation CD or something,

239
00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:05.250
and you just sit there and listen

240
00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:06.810
to the same thing every day.

241
00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:09.540
So there's no real kind of development of the practice,

242
00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.790
it's quite kind of static in a way.

243
00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.410
So I think that's one thing.

244
00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:18.060
I think what Rich, the co-founder and my very good friend

245
00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:21.481
has brought to this project is a degree of creativity,

246
00:10:21.481 --> 00:10:23.650
which has never really existed.

247
00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:26.187
If you look back at meditation, it's fine for people

248
00:10:26.187 --> 00:10:28.290
who are interested in that kind of thing,

249
00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.540
and don't mind images of lotus leaves

250
00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:33.030
and people sitting cross legged under a waterfall

251
00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:34.530
with a sunset in the background.

252
00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:37.830
But for most people, that's not really kind of their thing,

253
00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.720
and it's another barrier,

254
00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:44.460
and Rich has this ability to create a whole kind of world,

255
00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:47.850
a visual world, which is immediately engaging,

256
00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:51.630
which is nonthreatening, and which is very approachable.

257
00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:55.410
So I think those things, authenticity, creativity,

258
00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.870
and I'm gonna add in a third one, which is science,

259
00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:00.150
have all helped bridge that gap.

260
00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:02.940
So if you look at the science in the last 20 years

261
00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.183
around meditation and mindfulness, it's astonishing.

262
00:11:07.588 --> 00:11:11.010
In the past, simply, there was no way of knowing

263
00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.420
what was happening to the brain.

264
00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:13.770
So now we're not only talking about

265
00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:15.210
what happens to the mind,

266
00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.100
but we're talking about the physiological impact

267
00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.390
on the brain itself.

268
00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:22.050
And seeing that parts of the brain actually change

269
00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:23.760
not only in the amount of blood flow they get,

270
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:26.730
but they change in shape and size and strength

271
00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.620
depending on our meditation.

272
00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.800
That's an amazing thing, it's a really compelling,

273
00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:33.425
again, invitation to practice, I think.

274
00:11:33.425 --> 00:11:34.258
Right.

275
00:11:34.258 --> 00:11:36.720
I mean, it's really having a moment right now.

276
00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.610
There is a zeitgeist moment happening.

277
00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.410
I mean, you've devoted your life to this.

278
00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.120
This is a long time coming,

279
00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:45.150
but it's really a convergence of popular culture

280
00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:49.230
with your interest and your expertise and your authenticity

281
00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.350
to get to this place where now science is interested,

282
00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.120
and we're looking at neuroplasticity.

283
00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:57.000
And I think you had, read or heard you say that,

284
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.461
mentioned that there's maybe 30 or 50,000,

285
00:12:00.461 --> 00:12:04.180
or 3000, or three to 5,000 peer reviewed studies

286
00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:06.623
on the impact of meditation.
There's over 5,000.

287
00:12:07.517 --> 00:12:08.730
I mean, that's crazy

288
00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.440
there's that many
It's huge.

289
00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:11.273
studies out there.

290
00:12:11.273 --> 00:12:13.173
And it's happened so quickly.

291
00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.700
The early studies kind of go back maybe 35 years,

292
00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.460
but really sort of the ones involving FMRI machines

293
00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.370
where we can actually see what's going on in the brain

294
00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:27.090
during meditation, it's only in the last like 12, 15 years.

295
00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.340
So it's really kind of recent.

296
00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:32.350
But I do think that's sped up

297
00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.160
kind of this acceptance of it.

298
00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:36.510
Yeah well, it makes it palatable.

299
00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.250
Like oh, well if that guy said so,

300
00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:39.717
or, you know?
Exactly.

301
00:12:39.717 --> 00:12:41.550
And even in the corporate partnerships

302
00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.520
that you guys have done with Virgin Atlantic,

303
00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.910
it sort of gives it this perimeter of acceptability

304
00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:50.052
with the mainstream that allows people

305
00:12:50.052 --> 00:12:53.580
to sort of embrace it a little more readily, I think.

306
00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:55.710
I think it just gives you that stamp of approval

307
00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:58.410
and people trust big brands like Virgin,

308
00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.330
and they get on the plane and man,

309
00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.610
Virgin is saying it's okay, I'll give it a go.

310
00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:03.748
Right, right, right.

311
00:13:03.748 --> 00:13:06.391
And then you get to occasionally go to Necker Island.

312
00:13:06.391 --> 00:13:08.309
Yeah, I mean, there has to be an upside

313
00:13:08.309 --> 00:13:09.985
to sitting in this studio.

314
00:13:09.985 --> 00:13:11.830
You gotta get something out of this.

315
00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:13.230
That's so funny.

316
00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.210
Well, I wanna talk about your story,

317
00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:17.760
I know that you've told your story many, many times,

318
00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.800
but hopefully you'll indulge me a little bit

319
00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:23.040
because it's so interesting and so unique,

320
00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.563
this path to where you are right now.

321
00:13:26.812 --> 00:13:28.710
If it's of interest,

322
00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:30.930
to go, I'm happy to.
It's certainly of interest.

323
00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.900
You're always deflating, or deflecting this,

324
00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:37.260
But I think you, maybe because you've told it so many times,

325
00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:38.730
but I think for the average person

326
00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:43.140
to even have the opportunity to speak to somebody

327
00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:45.510
who's had this kind of unique experience that you've had,

328
00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:47.640
it's really fascinating.

329
00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:50.970
I genuinely believe, this isn't deflection.

330
00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.350
And I will come onto the story.

331
00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.413
I genuinely believe we all have a story to tell.

332
00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:59.820
It doesn't sound extraordinary to ourselves

333
00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:01.380
because we've lived it.

334
00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:02.277
And so we're used to it.

335
00:14:02.277 --> 00:14:05.103
And so it doesn't kind of sound unusual in any way.

336
00:14:06.898 --> 00:14:07.950
And that's what I love about people,

337
00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:09.780
and want to speak to other people.

338
00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:12.453
Everyone has a story, and even if people

339
00:14:12.453 --> 00:14:15.973
have stayed in one place their entire life,

340
00:14:15.973 --> 00:14:17.730
and been around the same people,

341
00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:19.953
there is an incredible story to tell.

342
00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:23.100
And mine just happens to have been,

343
00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.140
I guess it's quite varied.

344
00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:28.020
And it's been in a number of different places.

345
00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.850
But it starts with your mom

346
00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:31.681
being interested in meditation.

347
00:14:31.681 --> 00:14:33.090
It does, it does.
Encouraging you

348
00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:34.650
as a young lad.

349
00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:35.957
Yeah, actually, yeah.

350
00:14:35.957 --> 00:14:38.670
It actually starts with my folks getting divorced,

351
00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:40.980
going through quite a difficult period of time.

352
00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:42.750
My mom looking for a way to cope.

353
00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:46.967
And she decided to sign up for a course in meditation.

354
00:14:46.967 --> 00:14:49.350
It was actually TM back then.

355
00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:52.620
And my sister was going along and I was a bit put out

356
00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:55.980
that I hadn't been invited, I said, can I come along too?

357
00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:59.283
And that was the beginning of it.

358
00:15:00.358 --> 00:15:04.560
The very first time, I remember well, the very first time.

359
00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:07.590
I don't think I'd ever experienced a quiet mind before then.

360
00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:08.790
I don't think I did.

361
00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:11.670
Even as a kid, I had quite a busy mind.

362
00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.050
I was very active.

363
00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.880
I don't remember kind of experiencing

364
00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:16.710
that kind of silence before.

365
00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:19.140
And it left a real mark.

366
00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:21.720
From that one experience, or were you going back?

367
00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:23.130
Did this become a practice?

368
00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:24.690
Yeah, so it was a course.

369
00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:27.270
I forget, it was like about three months

370
00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:28.800
or something like that, the course.

371
00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:30.390
And we'd go back each week.

372
00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:33.240
I have to say, and this is very typical in meditation.

373
00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:34.500
I went along and I had

374
00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.784
such an amazing kind of first experience

375
00:15:36.784 --> 00:15:38.280
that I went back the next week,

376
00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:39.810
assuming that it would happen again

377
00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.850
and almost chasing the experience.

378
00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:42.960
And of course it didn't happen,

379
00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:44.370
'cause I was thinking too much

380
00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.080
about trying to recreate the experience

381
00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.840
rather than allowing the experience to happen.

382
00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:49.740
So it took a little while

383
00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.050
to kind of find the sweet spot there.

384
00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.420
But I kind of played around with it on and off

385
00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.253
for a good few years,

386
00:15:55.253 --> 00:15:59.565
I would say between like 10 and 13, something like that.

387
00:15:59.565 --> 00:16:03.210
And then you had this sort of rapid fire series

388
00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:05.520
of kind of unfortunate occurrences

389
00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.710
that kind of changed the trajectory

390
00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:08.617
of where you were headed.
Yeah.

391
00:16:08.617 --> 00:16:10.290
So it was actually quite a bit later,

392
00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.840
That was probably like more when I was like 18 or something.

393
00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.223
18, 19.

394
00:16:15.641 --> 00:16:18.060
Yeah, I would definitely attribute

395
00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:20.370
part of why I went away to this.

396
00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:22.530
And I was talking to a friend actually,

397
00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:25.203
who was there at the time, just earlier today.

398
00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:30.033
And we were just, it was Christmas Eve.

399
00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.740
We were stood outside of an old rugby club

400
00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:37.950
where we used to kind of play and we'd had a party there

401
00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:39.720
that night and everyone had had a few drinks,

402
00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:42.390
and we were just standing on the sidewalk.

403
00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:44.850
And a drunk driver came down the hill.

404
00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:46.620
At the time, myself and two friends

405
00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.050
just stepped away from the group,

406
00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:52.140
just to relieve ourselves against the wall.

407
00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:56.370
And the car with a guy, they say he was a drunk driver,

408
00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:59.970
he lost control and just plowed into the group.

409
00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:02.370
Killing two people and putting,

410
00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:05.493
I don't know, I think like 12 people in intensive care.

411
00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:07.557
It was heavy.

412
00:17:07.557 --> 00:17:09.123
That's intense.

413
00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:13.023
And, you know.

414
00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:17.253
In terms of the imprint it leaves on the memory.

415
00:17:18.508 --> 00:17:21.393
Not only the sight of it, but the sound of it.

416
00:17:21.393 --> 00:17:25.170
It's almost like, it's like, camera,

417
00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:28.560
like frames of a video or something, kind of shot.

418
00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:32.340
Everything is just, time is a very malleable thing.

419
00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:35.345
And in that moment, everything was seen and heard,

420
00:17:35.345 --> 00:17:39.647
in such a kind of shocking way that everyone dealt with it,

421
00:17:39.647 --> 00:17:41.772
I guess, in different ways.

422
00:17:41.772 --> 00:17:46.772
And for me, I just never really felt like I processed that.

423
00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:49.470
So I just carried it around with me instead

424
00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:51.180
for a number of years,

425
00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:53.970
until it kind of bubbled to the surface,

426
00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:55.950
wanting to be dealt with.

427
00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:58.290
Dealt with in a different way.

428
00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:01.520
And then you had, your stepsister was also hit by...

429
00:18:02.396 --> 00:18:05.550
Yeah, right after, about three months later,

430
00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:07.645
something like that, she was...

431
00:18:07.645 --> 00:18:08.550
On a bicycle?
A van, yeah,

432
00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:10.140
she was out cycling and a van driver

433
00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:14.730
fell asleep at the wheel, and very sadly she died.

434
00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:15.780
Right, so you're dealing

435
00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:19.500
with some compounded, heavy grief.

436
00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:22.590
Yeah, it was, and all mixed in with that still

437
00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:25.980
kind of growing up, trying to find

438
00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:27.903
kind of your place in life,

439
00:18:28.908 --> 00:18:32.280
and be accepted by peers and all the usual kind of stuff.

440
00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.006
Young man, a lot of hormones.

441
00:18:34.006 --> 00:18:34.982
Yeah.

442
00:18:34.982 --> 00:18:36.009
(Andy and Rich laughing)

443
00:18:36.009 --> 00:18:39.390
All that stuff. Yeah.

444
00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:40.502
And you're starting university,

445
00:18:40.502 --> 00:18:41.817
nonetheless,
Yes.

446
00:18:41.817 --> 00:18:43.680
and you're studying sports science?

447
00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.673
That's right, yeah.

448
00:18:45.673 --> 00:18:47.640
I took a few years away.

449
00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:49.950
In retrospect I see it was not only

450
00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:51.930
through a sense of adventure, it was probably also

451
00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:53.130
trying to get away a little bit.

452
00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.550
I went and worked a ski season in the Alps.

453
00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.200
Did a summer season in (indistinct),

454
00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:00.390
I came over here and worked in America a little bit,

455
00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:01.349
doing some summer camps.

456
00:19:01.349 --> 00:19:02.365
Oh, this is all before

457
00:19:02.365 --> 00:19:03.198
university?
This is all

458
00:19:03.198 --> 00:19:04.031
before university.

459
00:19:04.031 --> 00:19:04.980
So you have this wanderlust.

460
00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:06.450
This is already...
Official.

461
00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:09.060
Yeah, from a very, very early age.

462
00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:10.350
And then I went back to the UK

463
00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.870
and started a sports science degree.

464
00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.480
And I was about, I finished the first year,

465
00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:20.480
I was just into my second year when I had this moment of,

466
00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.351
why am I doing this?

467
00:19:22.351 --> 00:19:24.270
I'm not happy.

468
00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:25.967
It's a really funny thing 'cause,

469
00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:28.953
I was having a really good time.

470
00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.880
Student life is fun. It's a good time.

471
00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:34.230
And we were going out and we were doing

472
00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:36.383
all the things you do when you're a student.

473
00:19:37.366 --> 00:19:41.280
But it wasn't happiness at a deep level.

474
00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.100
There was no kind of sense of fulfillment.

475
00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:46.410
It was just kind of temporary sort of fun.

476
00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:49.230
So you wake up the next morning and if you're lucky,

477
00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:50.730
you can remember what happened the night before,

478
00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:54.210
but you're back where you were before, which is, wow.

479
00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:55.320
Back here again.

480
00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:58.371
And for me that was something that

481
00:19:58.371 --> 00:20:00.720
was kind of unsustainable.

482
00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:03.150
But that's a pretty heavy level of maturity

483
00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:06.030
to have at that age to be so introspective.

484
00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:08.302
I mean, sort of the typical...

485
00:20:08.302 --> 00:20:09.540
(Andy laughing)

486
00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:12.420
Like looking back, I could have that same

487
00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:14.400
dawning realization about my experience,

488
00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:16.650
but I certainly wasn't about to

489
00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.900
sort of wrestle with that at that time.

490
00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.605
So what do you think it was about you

491
00:20:21.605 --> 00:20:24.570
that you were really the one who actually was willing

492
00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:28.260
to look at it and actually take a contrary action?

493
00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:29.515
Well rarely in life

494
00:20:29.515 --> 00:20:30.939
have I been accused of being mature.

495
00:20:30.939 --> 00:20:31.980
(Rich laughing)

496
00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:33.450
But I'll take it.

497
00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:36.608
I mean, that is pretty mature.

498
00:20:36.608 --> 00:20:39.810
I do think I grew up in an environment

499
00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:42.333
where that wasn't considered kind of alien.

500
00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:48.270
Mom was, she was trained as as a counselor,

501
00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:49.913
as a hypnotherapist.
You had a hippie mom.

502
00:20:49.913 --> 00:20:51.766
A little bit.

503
00:20:51.766 --> 00:20:54.540
And we went and kind of,

504
00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.250
we went along to meditation classes

505
00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:57.180
and all that sort of stuff.

506
00:20:57.180 --> 00:21:00.330
I think it was there in the background.

507
00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:02.340
So in that sense, there was permission,

508
00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:04.080
even if it wasn't permission for my peers

509
00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.240
who thought I was completely mad.

510
00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:08.310
There was at least some permission from my family

511
00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:10.833
that this was an okay kind of thing to do.

512
00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:13.830
And at that particular time I was going out

513
00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:17.130
with a lovely girl at university

514
00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:18.963
who was heavily into Buddhism.

515
00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:22.390
Had just been telling me all about kind of

516
00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:25.593
these monks and nuns in the Himalayas.

517
00:21:27.029 --> 00:21:28.200
And the more I thought about it,

518
00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:32.160
the more I thought, that's a really good idea.

519
00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:34.905
And it was just, something kind of happened.

520
00:21:34.905 --> 00:21:37.080
It was one afternoon.

521
00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:38.940
Just something, I say something snapped,

522
00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:40.860
but something changed.

523
00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:45.860
And it changed with such a degree of clarity, uncertainty,

524
00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:48.865
that I just knew.

525
00:21:48.865 --> 00:21:51.630
I quit college that afternoon and left.

526
00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:52.830
That's amazing.

527
00:21:54.559 --> 00:21:56.407
Do you know Sam Harris?

528
00:21:56.407 --> 00:21:57.240
Yeah.

529
00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:59.490
Yeah, it's what Sam Harris did, kind of.

530
00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.710
When he was a freshman, and we were,

531
00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:05.460
I didn't know him, but we were in the same class in college.

532
00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.470
And he took off after freshman year

533
00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:08.970
on a similar sort of search.

534
00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:10.980
And now look at the two of you

535
00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:12.903
being sort of leading minds in this arena.

536
00:22:12.903 --> 00:22:14.760
Yeah I don't know Sam personally.

537
00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.520
I know of him and I know his story and yeah.

538
00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:19.590
A lot of similarities.

539
00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:23.700
So you head east. You head to India.

540
00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:24.533
I did.

541
00:22:24.533 --> 00:22:26.550
You went to a bunch of places.

542
00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:28.470
Yeah, I was actually gonna fly.

543
00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.690
The original plan was to go to Thailand

544
00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:32.340
and just straight to a particular monastery

545
00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:33.693
that I knew of there.

546
00:22:35.566 --> 00:22:38.340
And then I met someone who kind of said, oh yeah,

547
00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:39.960
my friend's just come back from this place

548
00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:41.940
in Northern India, the Dalai Lama lives there,

549
00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:42.990
it's an amazing place.

550
00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:44.880
And I thought, great.

551
00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.230
Sounds like a good place to start.

552
00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:47.820
So I went there first.

553
00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:48.900
Dharamshala?

554
00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.810
Yeah, Dharamshala, yeah, that's right.

555
00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:54.420
These days it's quite kind of touristy.

556
00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:57.990
Back then, it was a little less so, I would say.

557
00:22:57.990 --> 00:22:59.880
And I went there thinking, okay,

558
00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:01.470
well I'll find a Tibetan monastery,

559
00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:05.250
and I found the whole Tibetan thing completely overwhelming.

560
00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:07.216
It's so rich.

561
00:23:07.216 --> 00:23:09.573
There's so much stuff going on.

562
00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:12.960
Visually, rituals, and at the time,

563
00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:14.670
I just didn't understand it.

564
00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:17.880
And I was so complicated in my own mind.

565
00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:20.000
I was just really...

566
00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:23.580
I just wanted simplicity,

567
00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:25.440
Whether it's like that idea of Zen,

568
00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:27.240
where you just sit there and there's nothing.

569
00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:28.073
There's nothing.

570
00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:33.980
Or whether the more traditional types of Buddhism in Asia.

571
00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:38.190
So I ended up starting there, then I did,

572
00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:41.400
I studied about five years in the Burmese school.

573
00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:44.523
Not just in Burma, but with a teacher in Australia as well.

574
00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:46.863
And back in the UK.

575
00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:52.546
And I would say the Burmese tradition is quite strict.

576
00:23:52.546 --> 00:23:55.080
But it's a really good foundation.

577
00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:57.450
Strict in terms of the practice,

578
00:23:57.450 --> 00:23:59.474
and the sort of ascetic lifestyle?

579
00:23:59.474 --> 00:24:01.237
Yeah, everything.

580
00:24:01.237 --> 00:24:02.915
(Andy and Rich laughing)

581
00:24:02.915 --> 00:24:03.748
Everything.

582
00:24:03.748 --> 00:24:06.213
I actually found it quite challenging.

583
00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:10.478
There was I guess the adventurous part of me

584
00:24:10.478 --> 00:24:12.897
that had this idea of what it was,

585
00:24:12.897 --> 00:24:16.560
and I really loved the idea of it.

586
00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.720
But being in it, wow.

587
00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:19.680
It's quite hard.

588
00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:22.020
Is that when you're practicing to be ordained,

589
00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:23.580
or once you are ordained?
Yeah, So that was

590
00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:27.060
when I was a lay person and as a novice monk.

591
00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:27.893
I see.

592
00:24:27.893 --> 00:24:30.720
And so what's a day in the life like that?

593
00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:32.670
So it varies.

594
00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:34.710
As a lay person, it's a lot more varied.

595
00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:36.600
And you might not even be in the monastery.

596
00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:38.460
In the monastery as a novice monk.

597
00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:39.770
It's quite...

598
00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:42.540
It's quite full on, I'd say.

599
00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:46.113
So typically in Burma itself, if you're in retreat.

600
00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.743
The day starts at about 2:30.

601
00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:52.413
The meditation begins at 3:00.

602
00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:56.068
And in the particular one that I trained in,

603
00:24:56.068 --> 00:24:58.260
it was about 18 hours a day.

604
00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:01.440
So nine hours sitting, nine hours walking.

605
00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:03.210
One hour of food.

606
00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:04.380
Breakfast, five o'clock in the morning,

607
00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:07.110
one hour of food, lunch at 11 in the morning.

608
00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:09.870
And then about three and a half hours for sleep.

609
00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:10.703
And that was it.

610
00:25:10.703 --> 00:25:11.790
Wow.

611
00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:13.290
Nine hours of sitting and

612
00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:14.180
nine hours
Nine hours of walking.

613
00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:16.112
of walking meditation.

614
00:25:16.112 --> 00:25:17.211
So there's one big room.

615
00:25:17.211 --> 00:25:18.044
So very different from the Tibetan.

616
00:25:18.044 --> 00:25:20.880
So in the Burmese retreats, there's one big room,

617
00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:22.740
and everybody practices together.

618
00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:26.310
And you all sit down one side of the temple.

619
00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:28.920
And under little mosquito nets.

620
00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:31.440
And you do your hour.

621
00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:32.610
The gong goes.

622
00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:34.260
The mosquito nets come up,

623
00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:37.080
and you then walk just backwards and forwards, very slowly,

624
00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.120
doing walking meditation for an hour.

625
00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:41.400
And then the gong goes again, you sit down.

626
00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:43.050
So you're alternating hour, hour, hour.

627
00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:46.110
Exactly, whereas in the Tibetan retreats,

628
00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:47.790
the longer term retreats.

629
00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:50.190
It's maybe a little less, more like kind of 16 hours,

630
00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:53.160
but it might be sort of like four blocks of four hours

631
00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:55.607
throughout the day, just sitting and no walking.

632
00:25:55.607 --> 00:25:58.080
And no sort of like acclimation period.

633
00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:01.740
You go from sort of British school boy

634
00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:04.888
to 16, 18 hours a day.

635
00:26:04.888 --> 00:26:07.000
Yeah.

636
00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.103
And I kind of,

637
00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:11.280
I think that was part of the challenge.

638
00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:13.230
It's a bit kind of all or nothing,

639
00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:14.970
just kind of jumping in at the deep end.

640
00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:18.180
And when I look back now, I would,

641
00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:20.730
often now people will write in and ask me about it.

642
00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:22.140
They're keen to go off.

643
00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:24.787
And I advise people in a very different way.

644
00:26:24.787 --> 00:26:26.220
I'm like, look.

645
00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:30.123
Start off with a weekend retreat. Then try a week.

646
00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:32.940
If you're up for it, go away and do a month.

647
00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:34.620
If after a month or three months,

648
00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:37.590
you feel like that's the way to go, great.

649
00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:38.760
Jump in.

650
00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:41.070
But I wouldn't kind of jump straight in at the deep end.

651
00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:43.410
Right, and so how long were you doing,

652
00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:46.440
were you adhering to this rigorous of a...

653
00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:48.480
So again, that's kind of retreat.

654
00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:50.610
So living in the monastery is different from retreat.

655
00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:51.480
Living in a monastery,

656
00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:54.450
you might do six to eight hours of meditation a day.

657
00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:58.170
And then alongside that you're doing cooking and cleaning

658
00:26:58.170 --> 00:26:59.250
and that kind of thing.

659
00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:00.870
And then you'll go into retreat

660
00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:02.040
for a certain amount of time.

661
00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:05.493
The longest retreat that I did was a year.

662
00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:09.690
And you do that all day every day for a year.

663
00:27:09.690 --> 00:27:11.850
And is it silent retreat?

664
00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:13.290
That particular one.

665
00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:15.660
That particular one wasn't.

666
00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:18.570
I would say about five to six months of it was in silence.

667
00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:21.027
So depending on the techniques you're doing at the time,

668
00:27:21.027 --> 00:27:23.630
you might be in silence or not.

669
00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:26.907
But even when you're not silent,

670
00:27:26.907 --> 00:27:30.330
it's not like there's loads of spare time

671
00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:32.463
to just hang out and chat about the,

672
00:27:32.463 --> 00:27:33.687
and what are you gonna talk about?

673
00:27:33.687 --> 00:27:36.720
The latest movie that you didn't see?

674
00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:38.700
Are you writing letters

675
00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:40.950
back to your friends in England?

676
00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:43.350
How are they processing what you're doing?

677
00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:45.360
It sounds like your mom's all on board,

678
00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.240
but what about the rest of your family and your friends?

679
00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:49.680
Do they think you've gone off the deep end

680
00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:52.350
or are they, sort of, do they have your back?

681
00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:53.413
Are they encouraging you?

682
00:27:53.413 --> 00:27:56.280
I think mom was on board until

683
00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:58.200
it suddenly dawned on her

684
00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:00.180
that she may not have any grandchildren from me.

685
00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:03.348
And you didn't even talk about the celibacy.

686
00:28:03.348 --> 00:28:05.670
Then she was less on board.

687
00:28:05.670 --> 00:28:07.590
My dad was kind of getting on board.

688
00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:10.110
It took him a little longer, I would say, to,

689
00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:13.170
he was supportive always, but took him a lot longer

690
00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:14.580
to get on board.

691
00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:15.750
My friends.

692
00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.360
I think they were just genuinely bemused.

693
00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:22.340
Because they'd been going out drinking with this guy,

694
00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:24.278
like a few months earlier.

695
00:28:24.278 --> 00:28:25.913
And they're like, what is he doing?

696
00:28:26.757 --> 00:28:29.790
My lecturer at college probably summed it up.

697
00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:32.670
He was, when I went and told him, he was like, yeah,

698
00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:35.460
you should just go get some Prozac from the doctor.

699
00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:37.710
I think there was that kind of response,

700
00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:40.020
like oh, he's having a breakdown.

701
00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:41.651
He's lost it.

702
00:28:41.651 --> 00:28:43.830
Let's just let him go and do it sort of thing.

703
00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:45.690
Right, but at some point,

704
00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:48.960
maybe there's a little bit graver concern.

705
00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:52.230
We better go get him 'cause he might not come back.

706
00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:53.760
They never sent out any kind of

707
00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:56.760
intervention parties or anything like that.

708
00:28:56.760 --> 00:28:58.740
Maybe they talked about it and couldn't afford it.

709
00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:00.240
I don't know.

710
00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:03.870
But I have to say, my friends, I was writing back.

711
00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:05.400
Not loads from the monastery,

712
00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:08.280
but the times in between different monasteries,

713
00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:09.990
I definitely kept in touch.

714
00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:13.680
And I've gotta say my friends were incredibly supportive,

715
00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:16.080
and the longer the journey went along,

716
00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:17.787
the more supportive they became.

717
00:29:17.787 --> 00:29:19.710
Interesting. Interesting.

718
00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:23.100
So how long does it take for you to get officially ordained?

719
00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:26.520
So that was probably about five,

720
00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:28.290
maybe even six years into that journey

721
00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:31.595
to take full ordination in the Tibetan tradition.

722
00:29:31.595 --> 00:29:32.550
Yeah.

723
00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:34.380
And once you're ordained, how does that,

724
00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:37.530
does your lifestyle change or it's just, what happens now?

725
00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:39.530
It does change, it feels...

726
00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:42.390
Although it's all about impermanence,

727
00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:45.420
there's a greater feeling of permanence in the sense that,

728
00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:46.710
you've taken a commitment.

729
00:29:46.710 --> 00:29:49.263
And it's not necessarily for life.

730
00:29:50.372 --> 00:29:53.340
In the Tibetan tradition, typically you'll go along

731
00:29:53.340 --> 00:29:54.780
and they'll say, okay, do you want to take

732
00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:58.413
a commitment for three years, for five years, or life?

733
00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:00.433
And so you can do three years.

734
00:30:00.433 --> 00:30:01.266
And at the end of the three years,

735
00:30:01.266 --> 00:30:03.690
if you're like, hmm, I'd like to carry on,

736
00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:05.310
then you might do five years.

737
00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:07.860
So there's no kind of feeling of regret when you walk away.

738
00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:10.463
It's kind of, you've completed your period of training.

739
00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:17.960
Yeah, I've found it a real sense of relief actually.

740
00:30:18.060 --> 00:30:20.280
Making that commitment.

741
00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:21.420
There was no uncertainty.

742
00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:24.183
You know often when you're not sure which way to go in life?

743
00:30:24.183 --> 00:30:26.580
Like once you've made the decision,

744
00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:29.130
there's a sense of relief that comes with that.

745
00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:32.370
And I just felt, I felt really content and kind of happy

746
00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:33.990
in my direction in life.

747
00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:34.830
And that was,

748
00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:37.350
you made a three year commitment or the five year?

749
00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:40.506
Yeah, so I made, well,

750
00:30:40.506 --> 00:30:42.810
first I went and did a year in retreat.

751
00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:45.930
And then I did three, I did three years.

752
00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:46.763
Got you.

753
00:30:46.763 --> 00:30:49.650
And at this point, are you in Moscow already?

754
00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:51.363
How do you find your way there?

755
00:30:51.363 --> 00:30:55.170
So I took ordination in northern India,

756
00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:57.780
but then I went and spent some time in Moscow.

757
00:30:57.780 --> 00:30:59.040
So Moscow.

758
00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:00.180
But hold on a second.

759
00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:02.244
So, sorry.
(Andy laughing)

760
00:31:02.244 --> 00:31:04.500
In Northern India, I mean, were you in the Himalayas?

761
00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:06.741
Are you in the caves and having that kind of

762
00:31:06.741 --> 00:31:08.663
sort of experience that you hear about?

763
00:31:08.663 --> 00:31:11.430
Yeah, I wasn't in any cave,

764
00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:13.440
I was lucky there was a very nice

765
00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:17.880
Tibetan Buddhist monastery there, which was incredible.

766
00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:19.980
It's not, I wouldn't say it's set up for Westerners,

767
00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:21.570
but it's definitely a lot easier

768
00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:23.100
for Westerners to be there,

769
00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:25.440
and there's not too much cave dwelling

770
00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:26.700
that goes on these days.

771
00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:28.440
Yeah, I mean, you hear these,

772
00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:29.760
this is an interesting question.

773
00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.160
You hear these sort of mythic stories of these

774
00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:34.710
legendary sadhus who are in meditation

775
00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:37.410
for just incredibly long protracted periods of time

776
00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:39.990
where they're either not eating or they're being tended to

777
00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.813
by sort of people, and I'm wondering.

778
00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:46.320
Or, once they pass and leave their body,

779
00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:47.970
their bodies remain preserved.

780
00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:51.360
And I'm always trying to figure out the demarcation line

781
00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:53.670
between sort of truth and exaggeration here.

782
00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:55.410
And as someone who's lived there,

783
00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:57.783
what is your perspective on that?

784
00:31:58.802 --> 00:32:01.233
It's a really tricky one, I would say.

785
00:32:02.219 --> 00:32:06.300
I think there's a lot we don't understand about the mind.

786
00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:09.960
And I think a monastery is an incredible environment

787
00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:13.319
in which to go and understand more about the mind.

788
00:32:13.319 --> 00:32:16.680
I think there are definitely levels

789
00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:19.710
of understanding of consciousness,

790
00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:20.607
whatever you wanna call it.

791
00:32:20.607 --> 00:32:22.170
And I would say it's nothing

792
00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:24.150
kind of terribly mystical about it.

793
00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:27.903
I just, increasingly, the more time we spend in the present.

794
00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:31.800
Not only do the more calm do we feel,

795
00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:33.150
but the greater clarity there is,

796
00:32:33.150 --> 00:32:35.850
the greater the insight into the mind is.

797
00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:38.130
And with insight comes a different perspective

798
00:32:38.130 --> 00:32:39.880
and a different experience of life.

799
00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:42.813
As to some of those stories.

800
00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:48.480
I've never witnessed anyone having

801
00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:50.280
kind of passed on and their body being,

802
00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:52.553
I've certainly heard plenty of stories like that.

803
00:32:53.876 --> 00:32:56.310
All I would say is I met people there

804
00:32:56.310 --> 00:32:59.265
who had spent their entire life in meditation.

805
00:32:59.265 --> 00:33:00.810
Like their entire life.

806
00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:05.493
And they were extraordinary on every single level.

807
00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:09.420
I would be no more impressed were they able to do that

808
00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.490
after they died than if they were simply

809
00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:13.620
what they were when they were alive.

810
00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:17.340
I can remember walking into, there's one particular kind of,

811
00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:20.438
it's kinda like the SAS of meditators.

812
00:33:20.438 --> 00:33:22.290
The Seals.
Seal Team Six.

813
00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:23.280
Exactly.

814
00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:26.277
And they're taken out, they're chosen at a very early age,

815
00:33:26.277 --> 00:33:29.670
having a particular aptitude for meditation.

816
00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:32.100
And they're taken away.

817
00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:34.110
Of their own choice, but they're taken away

818
00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:35.760
from the monastery, away from the retreats,

819
00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:37.890
they live up in the mountains.

820
00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:42.870
And they spend their life dedicating it to meditation.

821
00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:46.860
And I went out of, very unusual, I had the very good fortune

822
00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:50.951
to go and meet the man who kind of runs this school

823
00:33:50.951 --> 00:33:53.280
up in the mountains.

824
00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:55.953
And it was just extraordinary.

825
00:33:57.990 --> 00:33:59.652
There was someone there.

826
00:33:59.652 --> 00:34:02.970
He was there, he was sat on the kind of couch

827
00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:04.140
as we went in the room,

828
00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:06.810
but it was as though no one was there.

829
00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:10.150
It was as though the entire room was just pure kind of

830
00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:14.070
compassion or love or space, whatever you wanna call it,

831
00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:15.150
it kind of doesn't matter.

832
00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:17.930
There was something otherworldly about it.

833
00:34:17.930 --> 00:34:19.383
It was quite extraordinary.

834
00:34:20.430 --> 00:34:24.215
And I'm a very down to earth bloke.

835
00:34:24.215 --> 00:34:27.570
I'm not big on kind of mystical kind of stuff,

836
00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:30.903
and hopefully that comes through in Headspace as a journey.

837
00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:35.070
But I have to say some of the people I met there

838
00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:36.300
were quite extraordinary

839
00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:38.730
and I've never met anyone like it in the West.

840
00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:40.080
Yeah, I can imagine.

841
00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:42.330
I think that, yeah, everything that you do

842
00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:44.998
is so grounded and relatable

843
00:34:44.998 --> 00:34:47.766
that maybe there's a reticence to even

844
00:34:47.766 --> 00:34:49.843
(laughs) go there.

845
00:34:49.843 --> 00:34:52.846
I just don't. I just don't.

846
00:34:52.846 --> 00:34:56.927
But I would imagine that surrounding yourself

847
00:34:56.927 --> 00:34:59.820
with that level of consciousness would make you

848
00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:04.410
start to kind of embrace a broader concept

849
00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:07.380
of our limited perspective of reality

850
00:35:07.380 --> 00:35:09.450
and how that relates to our abilities

851
00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:12.003
and our perceptions and all of that.

852
00:35:12.003 --> 00:35:13.680
I think what I saw in those teachers

853
00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:17.460
was a willingness to let go of self.

854
00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:19.680
They just weren't caught up in their own stuff,

855
00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:21.960
which meant there was just space for others.

856
00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:25.438
And that space, again, you can call it empathy,

857
00:35:25.438 --> 00:35:26.370
you can call it compassion.

858
00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:27.900
Doesn't really matter what we call it.

859
00:35:27.900 --> 00:35:31.560
But they were just always present for others.

860
00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:35.640
And in doing that, they seemed to always bring happiness

861
00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:37.167
and benefit to those around them.

862
00:35:37.167 --> 00:35:38.100
It was the extraordinary thing.

863
00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:40.170
Right. That's very cool.

864
00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:41.328
All right, so Moscow.

865
00:35:41.328 --> 00:35:43.260
(Andy and Rich laughing)

866
00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:45.180
Moscow, next on the map.
Yes.

867
00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:46.320
Yeah, Moscow came about,

868
00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:48.883
there was one particular teacher who fascinated me.

869
00:35:48.883 --> 00:35:51.150
He was an Irishman

870
00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:53.910
who had spent 12 years in cloistered retreat.

871
00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:57.123
So cloistered, no contact outside.

872
00:35:58.110 --> 00:36:01.530
And he'd done it at Tibetan Buddhist monastery in Scotland.

873
00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:05.670
And I was just fascinated by his journey,

874
00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:08.370
and I think very inspired by his journey,

875
00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:09.990
and it was a journey that I wanted

876
00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:12.300
to kind of recreate, I suppose.

877
00:36:12.300 --> 00:36:13.950
And I heard that he was living in Moscow

878
00:36:13.950 --> 00:36:15.810
and teaching in Moscow.

879
00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:18.210
And, so I moved to Moscow.

880
00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:19.800
It was really just seeking this guy out.

881
00:36:19.800 --> 00:36:21.396
Yeah.

882
00:36:21.396 --> 00:36:24.060
To get some teachings from him.

883
00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:27.420
And in the end we ended up, yeah, I stayed in Moscow,

884
00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:28.950
and then we went together to India

885
00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:32.340
and I took ordination as as a monk there.

886
00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:34.500
And then eventually kind of went back to Moscow

887
00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:38.640
and taught in the meditation center in Moscow.

888
00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:40.133
And what was that like living the life?

889
00:36:40.133 --> 00:36:43.590
I mean, it's a juxtaposition of so many bizarre

890
00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:45.720
things at once, you know?

891
00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:47.550
Just a monk alone, no matter where,

892
00:36:47.550 --> 00:36:51.190
but then a monk in Moscow, I can't imagine.

893
00:36:51.190 --> 00:36:53.760
That was, even now when I look back,

894
00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:56.790
I there's a part of me that goes, what were you doing?

895
00:36:56.790 --> 00:36:57.843
Seriously.

896
00:36:58.980 --> 00:37:02.091
I can remember, it was a time.

897
00:37:02.091 --> 00:37:03.930
Moscow's always a challenging place.

898
00:37:03.930 --> 00:37:05.970
I have to say, I love it as a city.

899
00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:08.520
It still kind of feels like home to me now.

900
00:37:08.520 --> 00:37:09.840
But I can remember walking down the street,

901
00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:12.120
I'd regularly get stopped by the police.

902
00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:15.450
They're not big fans of religious expression in Russia.

903
00:37:15.450 --> 00:37:17.430
And they'd be asking for papers and things.

904
00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:20.640
I can remember walking through the metro station one day,

905
00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:22.560
and a guy just running, charging at me

906
00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:23.910
and just rugby tackling me.

907
00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:26.787
He thought I was a Hare Krishna.

908
00:37:26.787 --> 00:37:29.790
So break down the attire though,

909
00:37:29.790 --> 00:37:32.280
are you wearing like a red skirt?

910
00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:34.950
Think the Dalai Lama kind of type outfit.

911
00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:36.600
So a maroon kind of/

912
00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:40.830
And so essentially for people who aren't familiar with that,

913
00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:44.327
it's a bald headed guy in a purple skirt.

914
00:37:44.327 --> 00:37:47.190
For people walking up and down the streets in Moscow,

915
00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:48.030
that's what they saw.

916
00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:50.760
They just saw a bald-headed guy in a purple skirt.

917
00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:52.290
And it was alien.

918
00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:54.870
And alien things tend to kind of freak us out a bit.

919
00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:56.310
It's so funny looking at you now,

920
00:37:56.310 --> 00:37:58.800
'cause you have your very trendy trainers on

921
00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:02.237
and you're very dapper and bespoke and all these things.

922
00:38:02.237 --> 00:38:04.470
Except for the bald head.

923
00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:08.550
Then I can see the trappings of the attire.

924
00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:09.540
But that's interesting.

925
00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:12.060
So how long were you in Moscow then?

926
00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:13.410
So I guess you were there twice, but the second time.

927
00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.780
Yeah, all together.

928
00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.670
I stayed thereafter then for probably about

929
00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:19.890
another two and a half years, something like that.

930
00:38:19.890 --> 00:38:21.750
But is that where you kind of,

931
00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:24.000
the end of your tenure as a monk

932
00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:25.647
sort of wound down?
Exactly.

933
00:38:25.647 --> 00:38:27.684
And I was undecided.

934
00:38:27.684 --> 00:38:30.396
I was genuinely not sure whether I was gonna

935
00:38:30.396 --> 00:38:32.280
keep doing what I was doing,

936
00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:34.560
or whether I was gonna change.

937
00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:37.310
And I actually went to see one of my teachers in India.

938
00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:42.777
And it was someone I had a huge amount of trust in.

939
00:38:42.777 --> 00:38:45.270
And I actually handed over the responsibility

940
00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:47.160
of the decision to him and I said, look,

941
00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:50.190
if you think I should do this for life, I'll do it for life.

942
00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:52.650
If you don't, I won't.

943
00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:53.880
And he said, well...

944
00:38:53.880 --> 00:38:57.053
That's a pretty big decision to suddenly

945
00:38:57.053 --> 00:38:58.890
sort of deputize somebody else

946
00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:00.437
to make on your behalf.
Yeah!

947
00:39:00.437 --> 00:39:01.744
(Andy and Rich laughing)

948
00:39:01.744 --> 00:39:02.640
Here, you can decide what I'm gonna do

949
00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:03.960
for the rest of my life.

950
00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:05.430
Some may say that's just avoiding

951
00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:08.130
the responsibility of making the decision.

952
00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:09.510
It's very hard to describe,

953
00:39:09.510 --> 00:39:12.030
but if you're in that practice every single day,

954
00:39:12.030 --> 00:39:14.700
and you have a particular teacher and a feeling of devotion

955
00:39:14.700 --> 00:39:16.980
or whatever towards that person,

956
00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.387
I genuinely believe that he knew better than I did

957
00:39:20.387 --> 00:39:22.290
in that moment.

958
00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:24.810
So I trusted him and his advice was to

959
00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:27.207
maybe not take life ordination just yet.

960
00:39:27.207 --> 00:39:28.680
And I went back to Moscow,

961
00:39:28.680 --> 00:39:31.887
and I was coming to the end of my sort of commitment there.

962
00:39:31.887 --> 00:39:34.560
And there were a number of people coming along

963
00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:36.480
to the meditation center.

964
00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:38.370
And a lot of them were lay people.

965
00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:41.490
Some of them were expats, some of them were Russian.

966
00:39:41.490 --> 00:39:46.490
And increasingly I saw that the way I was living my life

967
00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:52.250
was so often a obstacle in kind of presenting

968
00:39:53.550 --> 00:39:55.500
those teachings that I was trying to present.

969
00:39:55.500 --> 00:39:58.080
People would say, oh, it's easy for you, you're a monk.

970
00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:00.450
Wait until you've got a wife and screaming kids at home.

971
00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:04.095
Or, it's easy, you don't have any responsibilities,

972
00:40:04.095 --> 00:40:06.540
you don't have anything to kind of worry about.

973
00:40:06.540 --> 00:40:08.310
Or it might be the dress.

974
00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:10.050
And it's like, yeah.

975
00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:12.990
There are some people who find that a bit challenging

976
00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:14.340
to accept someone dressed like that,

977
00:40:14.340 --> 00:40:17.430
or the fact that I smelled of incense, whatever it might be.

978
00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:19.530
There were a number of potential obstacles.

979
00:40:19.530 --> 00:40:21.780
And the more I thought about it,

980
00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:24.300
the more I kind of thought, yeah, you know,

981
00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:28.290
maybe, just maybe if it was taken out of this setting,

982
00:40:28.290 --> 00:40:31.520
this could be so much more accessible.

983
00:40:31.520 --> 00:40:33.810
Yeah, I mean, I think that clearly

984
00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:34.923
this is your dharma.

985
00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:40.080
It's sort of ordained that you're meant to be

986
00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:40.950
doing what you're doing.

987
00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:45.120
But I wouldn't say that you're the first person

988
00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:47.910
who's come up with this idea of trying to translate

989
00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:50.550
these concepts for a mainstream society.

990
00:40:50.550 --> 00:40:54.540
So in that regard, how do you conceptualize

991
00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:57.990
what has made what you're doing successful?

992
00:40:57.990 --> 00:41:01.050
And if somebody were to ask me my opinion of that for you,

993
00:41:01.050 --> 00:41:04.380
I would say that you're an extraordinary communicator.

994
00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:07.503
You have an ability and agility and adeptness

995
00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:11.220
to be able to convey these principles,

996
00:41:11.220 --> 00:41:14.370
distill them down to their very core essence

997
00:41:14.370 --> 00:41:17.610
in a way that's sort of palatable.

998
00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:18.900
Not, palatable is the wrong word,

999
00:41:18.900 --> 00:41:23.900
but understandable and embraceable I suppose, by anybody.

1000
00:41:24.510 --> 00:41:27.573
Well, that's really nice to hear, and thank you.

1001
00:41:28.884 --> 00:41:33.884
I genuinely, I don't know, is the truth.

1002
00:41:34.549 --> 00:41:39.549
I think there is something in the experience.

1003
00:41:43.350 --> 00:41:45.810
People have asked me to look at other people's kind of

1004
00:41:45.810 --> 00:41:48.783
apps and material and things and say what do you think?

1005
00:41:49.747 --> 00:41:52.105
And everything, we all,

1006
00:41:52.105 --> 00:41:54.780
different things appeal to different people.

1007
00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:56.370
But for me personally,

1008
00:41:56.370 --> 00:41:59.760
I believe it's the authenticity of the experience.

1009
00:41:59.760 --> 00:42:01.470
I couldn't do what I'm doing

1010
00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:03.903
unless I had done what I'd done.

1011
00:42:04.890 --> 00:42:06.930
In this sort of Malcolm Gladwell-esque

1012
00:42:06.930 --> 00:42:10.200
10,000 hours, you've trumped that and then some

1013
00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:11.070
with the amount of hours

1014
00:42:11.070 --> 00:42:11.940
that you put into this.
Probably.

1015
00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:14.700
So you're speaking not only from direct experience,

1016
00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:17.820
but just a vast amount of that.

1017
00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:20.970
Yeah, I think there's something in that journey,

1018
00:42:20.970 --> 00:42:23.400
almost kind of cyclical.

1019
00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:25.530
It starts off very simple.

1020
00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:28.590
It gets quite kind of complicated at some stage.

1021
00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:32.010
And then it comes back around to being very simple again.

1022
00:42:32.010 --> 00:42:33.690
But there is an experience.

1023
00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:35.190
And without that experience in it,

1024
00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:37.470
without having kind of walked that,

1025
00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:42.090
it's very difficult to make it feel and sound simple again.

1026
00:42:42.090 --> 00:42:45.420
So when I read books, sometimes it just feels so

1027
00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:48.030
kind of complicated and unnecessarily complicated,

1028
00:42:48.030 --> 00:42:50.280
because we are only talking about the present moment,

1029
00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:54.960
and there is nothing complicated about the present moment.

1030
00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:59.910
But finding a way to approach that and talk about that,

1031
00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:02.370
I think, yeah, I just think that comes about

1032
00:43:02.370 --> 00:43:03.300
from experience.

1033
00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:04.133
Right

1034
00:43:04.133 --> 00:43:07.530
And so you return to the UK from Moscow

1035
00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:09.810
intent upon teaching.

1036
00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:12.750
Certainly, at first, initially one on one, is that...

1037
00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:14.640
It is.
Well, we skipped over

1038
00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:15.482
the whole circus thing.

1039
00:43:15.482 --> 00:43:16.704
Yeah, there was...

1040
00:43:16.704 --> 00:43:18.360
(Andy laughing)

1041
00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:21.123
Part of every monk's journey is the circus thing.

1042
00:43:22.396 --> 00:43:26.340
There was a bit of a segue, and it may sound really random.

1043
00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:28.563
And it may sound completely unrelated,

1044
00:43:29.610 --> 00:43:33.420
but it has become an integral part of what I'm doing now.

1045
00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:34.500
So I was in Moscow.

1046
00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:37.410
I had six months left to run of my time in Moscow,

1047
00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:39.060
but I wasn't a monk.

1048
00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:41.340
As a monk, you can't do kind of physical exercise,

1049
00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:44.130
you can't go and play games and things.

1050
00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:47.160
And yet before I was a monk, I competed as a gymnast

1051
00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:49.710
and I was a very kind of active person.

1052
00:43:49.710 --> 00:43:52.020
And a guy in Moscow I knew was going along

1053
00:43:52.020 --> 00:43:56.040
to Moscow State Circus and doing a degree in circus arts.

1054
00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:57.810
And he said, look, why don't you come along with me?

1055
00:43:57.810 --> 00:44:01.140
And so I went along and I was just hooked.

1056
00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:02.520
I absolutely loved it.

1057
00:44:02.520 --> 00:44:04.440
I'd been very quiet and very,

1058
00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:06.630
for such a long period of time, very introverted.

1059
00:44:06.630 --> 00:44:08.430
And all of a sudden there was an opportunity

1060
00:44:08.430 --> 00:44:10.623
to express something physically again.

1061
00:44:12.000 --> 00:44:13.830
And as a monk, I had nothing.

1062
00:44:13.830 --> 00:44:16.890
I'd given everything away. Even my clothes I'd given away.

1063
00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:17.723
So I was thinking, okay,

1064
00:44:17.723 --> 00:44:20.640
how am I gonna go back to England and start this thing

1065
00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:21.633
that I want to do?

1066
00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:24.060
Where am I gonna live?

1067
00:44:24.060 --> 00:44:25.383
How am I gonna pay for it?

1068
00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:29.130
And someone mentioned to me that you could go back to,

1069
00:44:29.130 --> 00:44:32.040
in London, you could do a degree in circus arts.

1070
00:44:32.040 --> 00:44:35.070
And as I'd be classed as a mature student, I'd get a grant,

1071
00:44:35.070 --> 00:44:36.870
I'd get a loan from the government.

1072
00:44:36.870 --> 00:44:38.580
I thought, this ticks all the boxes.

1073
00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:41.100
I can go and do something that makes me feel

1074
00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:43.530
a bit more kind of worldly again.

1075
00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:46.320
I can find a way to express this stuff.

1076
00:44:46.320 --> 00:44:48.510
And at the same time, on a very practical level,

1077
00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:52.080
I could afford to go back to England and live there.

1078
00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:52.913
Right.

1079
00:44:52.913 --> 00:44:55.728
And did the circus art thing though, kind of began in,

1080
00:44:55.728 --> 00:44:57.660
that was sort of swirling about you

1081
00:44:57.660 --> 00:44:58.637
in Moscow, right?
In Moscow, yeah.

1082
00:44:58.637 --> 00:45:00.450
'Cause that's kind of a big thing there,

1083
00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:01.320
isn't it?
It's massive.

1084
00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:02.820
So I probably spent about six months

1085
00:45:02.820 --> 00:45:05.730
having one to one classes at Moscow State Circus,

1086
00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:08.947
and then went and did a degree in London.

1087
00:45:08.947 --> 00:45:11.490
And your friends are now saying what?

1088
00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:14.580
They're like, well, he came back, but...

1089
00:45:14.580 --> 00:45:17.485
I think, my poor parents.

1090
00:45:17.485 --> 00:45:20.370
They just started to accept the fact that, okay,

1091
00:45:20.370 --> 00:45:22.263
he's chosen this path in life.

1092
00:45:23.130 --> 00:45:25.620
They suddenly started to feel kind of proud

1093
00:45:25.620 --> 00:45:27.780
and telling their friends, yeah, this is what,

1094
00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:29.340
and then it was, oh no,

1095
00:45:29.340 --> 00:45:32.760
he's now a clown in London.
He's really lost the thread.

1096
00:45:32.760 --> 00:45:34.680
So there was definitely,

1097
00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:37.110
my friends just laughed about it, by that stage,

1098
00:45:37.110 --> 00:45:40.382
I think they were just like, that's just his life.

1099
00:45:40.382 --> 00:45:42.267
They didn't think much about it.

1100
00:45:42.267 --> 00:45:44.850
And so you're learning to juggle

1101
00:45:44.850 --> 00:45:45.930
and tight rope walk,

1102
00:45:45.930 --> 00:45:48.913
and trapeze, all that stuff.
And trapeze, the whole,

1103
00:45:48.913 --> 00:45:50.790
everything you can.
That's so cool.

1104
00:45:50.790 --> 00:45:52.890
Just swinging around like a monkey

1105
00:45:52.890 --> 00:45:54.184
for nine hours a day, five days a week.

1106
00:45:54.184 --> 00:45:55.200
Right.

1107
00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:56.790
And this is kind of like where you go,

1108
00:45:56.790 --> 00:45:58.585
if you want to be in Cirque du Soleil

1109
00:45:58.585 --> 00:46:00.897
or the traditional circus, I suppose.

1110
00:46:00.897 --> 00:46:03.030
Yeah, it was not so much traditional,

1111
00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:04.170
it was a bit more contemporary,

1112
00:46:04.170 --> 00:46:06.570
so the emphasis was on physical theater.

1113
00:46:06.570 --> 00:46:09.270
So a lot of dance and a lot of theater.

1114
00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:11.460
And for me, this is where it becomes relevant.

1115
00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:13.729
If I'd have gone straight from the monastery,

1116
00:46:13.729 --> 00:46:18.030
there is no way in the world that I would feel comfortable

1117
00:46:18.030 --> 00:46:22.323
standing up in front of big audiences and doing what we do.

1118
00:46:23.749 --> 00:46:24.930
In the circus it was amazing.

1119
00:46:24.930 --> 00:46:26.910
'Cause you had to get up on the stage

1120
00:46:26.910 --> 00:46:29.340
and they'd just say, okay, make us laugh.

1121
00:46:29.340 --> 00:46:31.890
No material. You can't tell any jokes.

1122
00:46:31.890 --> 00:46:33.693
Just physical, just make us laugh.

1123
00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:37.230
And often, it wouldn't work.

1124
00:46:37.230 --> 00:46:39.510
Sometimes it would, and that feeling's amazing when it does,

1125
00:46:39.510 --> 00:46:40.500
but often it wouldn't work.

1126
00:46:40.500 --> 00:46:42.930
And they'd just be okay, go.

1127
00:46:42.930 --> 00:46:45.500
Until it kind of got to a point where

1128
00:46:45.500 --> 00:46:48.600
you are no longer afraid to fail.

1129
00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:52.830
And again, that's a really liberating place to be,

1130
00:46:52.830 --> 00:46:55.770
to be able to go out there and do what you need to do

1131
00:46:55.770 --> 00:46:57.780
in front of other people,

1132
00:46:57.780 --> 00:47:01.770
but no longer be constrained by fear.

1133
00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:03.510
Instead just doing what you do

1134
00:47:03.510 --> 00:47:05.490
because you're doing what you're doing,

1135
00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:07.500
and being okay with that.

1136
00:47:07.500 --> 00:47:11.700
And I have found that just an incredibly useful thing

1137
00:47:11.700 --> 00:47:13.080
in what we do at Headspace.

1138
00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:16.110
This plays into my great communicator theory.

1139
00:47:16.110 --> 00:47:17.177
I think when you're,
I'm sure it's part of it.

1140
00:47:17.177 --> 00:47:19.710
if you watch your Ted talk or any of your talks,

1141
00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:23.660
it's the comfort level that you have is noticeable.

1142
00:47:23.660 --> 00:47:25.653
Yeah, and I really enjoy it.

1143
00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:29.220
I really enjoy the conversation.

1144
00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:31.740
What's also interesting is that

1145
00:47:31.740 --> 00:47:35.430
it's this juxtaposition of the interior and the exterior.

1146
00:47:35.430 --> 00:47:37.950
Because you had this life that was so focused

1147
00:47:37.950 --> 00:47:39.660
on the interior for so long,

1148
00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:43.116
and then you step into this very physical world to kind of,

1149
00:47:43.116 --> 00:47:45.750
and there's a, I would imagine that in that sort of

1150
00:47:45.750 --> 00:47:47.220
looking in the rear view mirror,

1151
00:47:47.220 --> 00:47:49.620
that was the counterbalance in this yin yang

1152
00:47:49.620 --> 00:47:50.520
kind of dance.

1153
00:47:50.520 --> 00:47:51.360
a Absolutely.

1154
00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:54.060
That makes you a more well formed person,

1155
00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:56.810
I would imagine, to be able to kind of be in the world.

1156
00:47:58.410 --> 00:48:01.190
I really needed something to kind of,

1157
00:48:01.190 --> 00:48:03.330
to make me feel more grounded

1158
00:48:03.330 --> 00:48:05.672
and to find a sense of humor again.

1159
00:48:05.672 --> 00:48:07.200
I wouldn't say I lost it in the monastery,

1160
00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:10.530
but there's not a lot of fooling around laughing.

1161
00:48:10.530 --> 00:48:11.430
Cracking jokes.

1162
00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:12.263
There's not.

1163
00:48:12.263 --> 00:48:14.083
I wish there was more.

1164
00:48:14.083 --> 00:48:16.202
There was a little bit.
It's pretty funny.

1165
00:48:16.202 --> 00:48:17.520
(Andy laughing)

1166
00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:20.480
There was some, there was some, but,

1167
00:48:20.480 --> 00:48:22.860
it did, it just balanced everything out.

1168
00:48:22.860 --> 00:48:26.005
And while I was there studying,

1169
00:48:26.005 --> 00:48:30.360
I used to get up at 4:00, 4:30 every morning.

1170
00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:31.350
And I would write.

1171
00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:32.760
So you slept in.

1172
00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:34.230
'Cause this is, you're getting to sleep,

1173
00:48:34.230 --> 00:48:36.012
you get two extra hours of sleep.

1174
00:48:36.012 --> 00:48:37.470
It's true, it's like a lie-in.

1175
00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:39.180
It's like a lie-in, you're right.

1176
00:48:39.180 --> 00:48:40.710
You're right, but I was writing content.

1177
00:48:40.710 --> 00:48:43.530
And I was writing the content which is now Headspace.

1178
00:48:43.530 --> 00:48:47.460
So weekends, mornings, evenings, I would just be writing.

1179
00:48:47.460 --> 00:48:49.560
And so there was never an idea

1180
00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:50.910
that you were going to try to be

1181
00:48:50.910 --> 00:48:52.620
a professional circus performer,

1182
00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:54.709
this was just something that was interesting to you,

1183
00:48:54.709 --> 00:48:56.910
but the intention was always

1184
00:48:56.910 --> 00:48:59.850
to teach these principles that you had learned.

1185
00:48:59.850 --> 00:49:00.683
Absolutely.

1186
00:49:00.683 --> 00:49:03.750
I think there was a time, maybe like a year in,

1187
00:49:03.750 --> 00:49:06.205
where I was just enjoying it so much.

1188
00:49:06.205 --> 00:49:08.190
I kind of thought, let's see,

1189
00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:11.130
I suppose a little bit of work wouldn't be a bad thing.

1190
00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:14.790
But it was never, I was far too old to be a...

1191
00:49:14.790 --> 00:49:17.400
At this at this stage, I was 32

1192
00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:19.560
when I started the degree.

1193
00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:22.253
They even made me sign a special form to say, look,

1194
00:49:22.253 --> 00:49:24.390
I'm probably gonna get injured, if I do,

1195
00:49:24.390 --> 00:49:25.680
it's not your fault.

1196
00:49:25.680 --> 00:49:27.840
The rest of the kids were like, they were 18 years old.

1197
00:49:27.840 --> 00:49:30.870
What is the application process?

1198
00:49:30.870 --> 00:49:32.280
Do you have to go in and perform?

1199
00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:33.450
You do.

1200
00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:36.660
But I was in Moscow, so they let me do a video audition.

1201
00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:39.030
So I had some friends in Moscow with a video.

1202
00:49:39.030 --> 00:49:39.863
And we...

1203
00:49:39.863 --> 00:49:41.490
So you're juggling while like

1204
00:49:41.490 --> 00:49:43.263
walking on a bouncing ball or something like that?

1205
00:49:43.263 --> 00:49:46.226
That kind of thing. That kind of thing.

1206
00:49:46.226 --> 00:49:47.985
It's very interesting.

1207
00:49:47.985 --> 00:49:50.430
All right, so you're beginning to teach.

1208
00:49:50.430 --> 00:49:52.950
And my understanding is that it's sort of something

1209
00:49:52.950 --> 00:49:55.770
that goes from the one on one to small groups,

1210
00:49:55.770 --> 00:49:57.090
to larger groups.

1211
00:49:57.090 --> 00:50:00.840
And ultimately to kind of through your partner, Rich.

1212
00:50:00.840 --> 00:50:01.830
Yeah.

1213
00:50:01.830 --> 00:50:04.950
Beginning to embrace the idea of using the web

1214
00:50:04.950 --> 00:50:06.690
to kind of scale what you're doing.

1215
00:50:06.690 --> 00:50:07.650
Yeah, so I was actually doing

1216
00:50:07.650 --> 00:50:09.060
one to one in a clinic.

1217
00:50:09.060 --> 00:50:11.970
So it was a mainstream kind of clinic where people would go

1218
00:50:11.970 --> 00:50:13.290
if they were struggling with sleeping

1219
00:50:13.290 --> 00:50:15.210
or high blood pressure or anxiety,

1220
00:50:15.210 --> 00:50:16.950
and often it would be alongside

1221
00:50:16.950 --> 00:50:19.023
traditional kind of medical treatment.

1222
00:50:19.950 --> 00:50:22.770
Sometimes people self referred and would come along.

1223
00:50:22.770 --> 00:50:24.210
And I met Rich.

1224
00:50:24.210 --> 00:50:27.150
Rich was just burnt out from working in advertising.

1225
00:50:27.150 --> 00:50:28.770
Just completely burnt out,

1226
00:50:28.770 --> 00:50:31.620
and wanted to learn how to meditate.

1227
00:50:31.620 --> 00:50:33.900
And at the time I knew I wanted to do something

1228
00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:37.530
more than one to one, but I didn't know how to do it.

1229
00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:39.180
So we did a skill swap,

1230
00:50:39.180 --> 00:50:41.910
and Rich would come along for an hour at the clinic.

1231
00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:43.200
We'd do a session.

1232
00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:45.600
And then we'd pop across the road to the coffee shop,

1233
00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:47.820
and he'd give me marketing 101.

1234
00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:52.812
And after meeting up, repeat about three or four months,

1235
00:50:52.812 --> 00:50:56.280
we both knew that there was something there,

1236
00:50:56.280 --> 00:50:59.566
and he was really excited about the opportunity, I was.

1237
00:50:59.566 --> 00:51:00.630
And so we started to talk about,

1238
00:51:00.630 --> 00:51:02.040
okay, what does it look like?

1239
00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:05.250
We spent probably about a year working on just the brand.

1240
00:51:05.250 --> 00:51:07.140
So what does the brand do? What does it feel like?

1241
00:51:07.140 --> 00:51:08.250
How does it talk?

1242
00:51:08.250 --> 00:51:11.561
'Cause brands hadn't really existed in meditation before.

1243
00:51:11.561 --> 00:51:13.110
And your idea was,

1244
00:51:13.110 --> 00:51:15.750
well, we must have this orange ball.

1245
00:51:15.750 --> 00:51:17.100
That was the main idea.

1246
00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:19.192
I think that was that was the principal.

1247
00:51:19.192 --> 00:51:20.490
This is what you're bringing to the equation.

1248
00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:22.140
Actually, that would make more sense than,

1249
00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:24.810
our original idea was this, our brief for it was,

1250
00:51:24.810 --> 00:51:28.680
how do we create something that we could

1251
00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:30.630
talk about to our mates in the pub,

1252
00:51:30.630 --> 00:51:32.399
without them laughing about it?

1253
00:51:32.399 --> 00:51:34.800
Now that was the brief for Headspace.

1254
00:51:34.800 --> 00:51:38.040
And, maybe there's still a little bit of that

1255
00:51:38.040 --> 00:51:39.243
in there now as well.

1256
00:51:40.500 --> 00:51:42.210
And where does the orange ball come from?

1257
00:51:42.210 --> 00:51:43.510
Is that from the juggling?

1258
00:51:44.670 --> 00:51:46.230
It should be, right?

1259
00:51:46.230 --> 00:51:49.013
That was a complete coincidence I think.

1260
00:51:49.013 --> 00:51:51.600
Originally, I don't know if you saw the old logo,

1261
00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:54.330
it was actually a person sitting on a chair,

1262
00:51:54.330 --> 00:51:56.940
and it was just how, like a feeling of Headspace.

1263
00:51:56.940 --> 00:51:59.610
So the ball was actually their head,

1264
00:51:59.610 --> 00:52:01.530
and it was sitting on top of the body,

1265
00:52:01.530 --> 00:52:04.140
and we had about four different colors for it.

1266
00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:07.080
And eventually as usually happens,

1267
00:52:07.080 --> 00:52:08.940
it just gets distilled and simplified,

1268
00:52:08.940 --> 00:52:11.730
and yeah, and it became just the orange dot.

1269
00:52:11.730 --> 00:52:12.780
Interesting.

1270
00:52:13.942 --> 00:52:17.880
I think that, back to this idea of kind of

1271
00:52:17.880 --> 00:52:21.390
the postmodern autobiography of a yogi,

1272
00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:23.760
you look at Yogananda's life,

1273
00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:26.670
and he emigrates to the United States with this idea

1274
00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:30.262
of trying to translate these principles of yoga

1275
00:52:30.262 --> 00:52:33.087
and teach them to a Western audience.

1276
00:52:33.087 --> 00:52:34.230
And so what does he do?

1277
00:52:34.230 --> 00:52:37.800
Well, he creates a bit of a following,

1278
00:52:37.800 --> 00:52:40.920
and then he starts to construct these edifices,

1279
00:52:40.920 --> 00:52:43.979
these ashrams, these giant, they're beautiful.

1280
00:52:43.979 --> 00:52:45.510
In some of the best surf spots in the world.

1281
00:52:45.510 --> 00:52:46.980
Exactly, Swami's in Encinitas.

1282
00:52:46.980 --> 00:52:47.813
Exactly.

1283
00:52:47.813 --> 00:52:48.870
That's why it's called Swami's.

1284
00:52:48.870 --> 00:52:50.280
Seriously.

1285
00:52:50.280 --> 00:52:51.393
If he hadn't built on there,

1286
00:52:51.393 --> 00:52:52.530
there might be a Headspace construction

1287
00:52:52.530 --> 00:52:54.296
going on down there right now.
Yeah, right.

1288
00:52:54.296 --> 00:52:57.360
And of course the beautiful one in the Palisades,

1289
00:52:57.360 --> 00:53:01.412
which are wonderful, but in 2015,

1290
00:53:01.412 --> 00:53:03.960
that's kind of an outdated modality.

1291
00:53:03.960 --> 00:53:08.340
So it's sort of, what was once the ashram is now the app.

1292
00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:11.040
Because the app is the forum

1293
00:53:11.040 --> 00:53:13.590
around which groups can congregate,

1294
00:53:13.590 --> 00:53:18.093
and that can be done, liberated from geography.

1295
00:53:19.582 --> 00:53:22.497
The app is your ashram, really.

1296
00:53:22.497 --> 00:53:24.243
Yeah.

1297
00:53:24.243 --> 00:53:26.130
Not to use that term,

1298
00:53:26.130 --> 00:53:27.600
because I know that you don't wanna,

1299
00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:30.300
you're trying to avoid that kind of thing.

1300
00:53:30.300 --> 00:53:33.390
But I think it's an apt analogy.

1301
00:53:33.390 --> 00:53:36.030
I think for most people, realistically,

1302
00:53:36.030 --> 00:53:38.280
given the amount of commitments and responsibilities

1303
00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:39.330
they have in their life,

1304
00:53:39.330 --> 00:53:42.831
it's highly unusual to be able to kind of disappear

1305
00:53:42.831 --> 00:53:46.470
off to some mountaintop and learn meditation.

1306
00:53:46.470 --> 00:53:48.406
And there's no need to.

1307
00:53:48.406 --> 00:53:51.537
I kind of would never have believed that before I went.

1308
00:53:51.537 --> 00:53:53.551
But genuinely.

1309
00:53:53.551 --> 00:53:56.970
The present moment is with us wherever we are in the world,

1310
00:53:56.970 --> 00:53:58.260
we don't need to go to the Himalayas

1311
00:53:58.260 --> 00:53:59.760
to experience the present moment.

1312
00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:01.920
So if there is a way of bringing those teachings

1313
00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:04.710
from the Himalayas, and kind of making them accessible

1314
00:54:04.710 --> 00:54:07.290
to somebody when they, I don't know,

1315
00:54:07.290 --> 00:54:08.688
they're sitting in the car park at work

1316
00:54:08.688 --> 00:54:10.230
before they go in at the morning,

1317
00:54:10.230 --> 00:54:11.370
or they're sitting on the train

1318
00:54:11.370 --> 00:54:12.990
going into work in the morning,

1319
00:54:12.990 --> 00:54:14.400
or they're at home after the kids

1320
00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:16.320
have just gone to sleep for their morning nap,

1321
00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:17.493
whatever it might be.

1322
00:54:18.480 --> 00:54:23.480
It just seems like such an incredible precious opportunity.

1323
00:54:23.580 --> 00:54:25.796
Or people often say there's a paradox.

1324
00:54:25.796 --> 00:54:29.490
Meditation on a phone? Does that make sense?

1325
00:54:29.490 --> 00:54:31.230
And absolutely it makes sense.

1326
00:54:31.230 --> 00:54:33.150
That's where people are right now.

1327
00:54:33.150 --> 00:54:35.580
So that's the place to kind of meet them I think.

1328
00:54:35.580 --> 00:54:38.070
Yeah, I mean, there is that sort of inherent irony

1329
00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:40.350
because it's about getting off your phone,

1330
00:54:40.350 --> 00:54:43.170
but it's using your phone to be more mindful

1331
00:54:43.170 --> 00:54:44.970
about your use of the phone, I suppose.

1332
00:54:44.970 --> 00:54:46.470
I mean, it's just a tool.

1333
00:54:46.470 --> 00:54:47.346
Whether you're using it

1334
00:54:47.346 --> 00:54:49.982
for self-improvement or other things.

1335
00:54:49.982 --> 00:54:51.960
Yeah. Yeah.

1336
00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:53.100
But it's this idea,

1337
00:54:53.100 --> 00:54:56.370
in the same way that your logo was distilled down

1338
00:54:56.370 --> 00:55:01.370
from a head with a balloon on it or whatever,

1339
00:55:01.890 --> 00:55:03.480
getting distilled down just the circle,

1340
00:55:03.480 --> 00:55:05.520
it's this distillation of these principles

1341
00:55:05.520 --> 00:55:09.480
and these teachings down to their very sort of core essence,

1342
00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:14.010
and then trying to relate those to a Western audience.

1343
00:55:14.010 --> 00:55:17.443
Is there sort of a,

1344
00:55:17.443 --> 00:55:21.180
what is the reception in a more traditional,

1345
00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:22.770
like what are your fellow monks

1346
00:55:22.770 --> 00:55:25.260
or people that know what you're doing, I mean, do they say,

1347
00:55:25.260 --> 00:55:27.990
oh, you're bastardizing this.

1348
00:55:27.990 --> 00:55:30.480
I mean, when authenticity is everything.

1349
00:55:30.480 --> 00:55:35.480
What is, is there some level of de minimus sacrifice

1350
00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:38.460
that takes place by not doing it the way

1351
00:55:38.460 --> 00:55:40.620
that you were actually taught?

1352
00:55:40.620 --> 00:55:42.914
That is the risk I think.

1353
00:55:42.914 --> 00:55:45.060
And I would say that's what's kind of

1354
00:55:45.060 --> 00:55:46.440
kept us honest in a way.

1355
00:55:46.440 --> 00:55:49.410
Kind of recognizing that there is that risk.

1356
00:55:49.410 --> 00:55:53.010
And working really hard to maintain that authenticity.

1357
00:55:53.010 --> 00:55:56.370
When I first set about doing it,

1358
00:55:56.370 --> 00:56:01.370
I would say the fear level was quite high in that community

1359
00:56:01.530 --> 00:56:03.973
of, oh my god, what's he doing?

1360
00:56:03.973 --> 00:56:07.038
And definitely when Rich and I started.

1361
00:56:07.038 --> 00:56:11.760
All the stuff we would get, like negative stuff,

1362
00:56:11.760 --> 00:56:13.950
would always be from people in the Buddhist community.

1363
00:56:13.950 --> 00:56:14.783
Always.

1364
00:56:14.783 --> 00:56:16.440
Even when we just ran events in London.

1365
00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:18.963
How dare you charge people to come to an event?

1366
00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:21.750
Not recognizing that actually,

1367
00:56:21.750 --> 00:56:25.620
if you go to any Buddhist center anywhere these days,

1368
00:56:25.620 --> 00:56:26.700
they charge you, they have to.

1369
00:56:26.700 --> 00:56:29.543
Because otherwise they can't afford to keep the place open.

1370
00:56:30.450 --> 00:56:33.330
So definitely there was some kind of concern.

1371
00:56:33.330 --> 00:56:36.120
And it's changed over the years.

1372
00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:40.320
And I have to say, for me that's a really,

1373
00:56:40.320 --> 00:56:42.723
personally, that's a really important thing,

1374
00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:45.540
to have the support of my teachers.

1375
00:56:45.540 --> 00:56:47.760
That's a really important thing.

1376
00:56:47.760 --> 00:56:50.070
And I've chatted to a couple of people recently,

1377
00:56:50.070 --> 00:56:52.530
there was a researcher we were talking to.

1378
00:56:52.530 --> 00:56:55.350
And he said that a friend of his who's been,

1379
00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:57.570
he was a monk and he'd been meditating for about 30 years,

1380
00:56:57.570 --> 00:56:59.490
runs a meditation center.

1381
00:56:59.490 --> 00:57:01.740
And now whenever anyone goes there,

1382
00:57:01.740 --> 00:57:04.350
he gets them away from the weekly session.

1383
00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:06.510
He gets them to use Headspace.

1384
00:57:06.510 --> 00:57:07.980
Somebody came back from Nepal,

1385
00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:09.330
and they'd gone to a monastery

1386
00:57:09.330 --> 00:57:11.340
where a lot of Westerners go to.

1387
00:57:11.340 --> 00:57:12.750
And when they go there,

1388
00:57:12.750 --> 00:57:15.690
before they do any kind of the longer meditations,

1389
00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:17.340
they're advised to use Headspace.

1390
00:57:18.570 --> 00:57:21.690
So I don't know if that kind of says anything or not,

1391
00:57:21.690 --> 00:57:25.012
but there's a sense, at least internally here,

1392
00:57:25.012 --> 00:57:27.870
that we are not misrepresenting the teachings

1393
00:57:27.870 --> 00:57:30.480
and have somehow managed to maintain

1394
00:57:30.480 --> 00:57:33.510
that sense of lineage or authenticity.

1395
00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:34.740
Yeah, that's beautiful.

1396
00:57:34.740 --> 00:57:38.430
I mean, that's gotta be kind of a tight rope walk, right?

1397
00:57:38.430 --> 00:57:40.309
Yeah, it's delicate.

1398
00:57:40.309 --> 00:57:41.930
It's really delicate.
Yeah, for sure.

1399
00:57:41.930 --> 00:57:45.420
And I think it's always open to this idea

1400
00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:47.418
that if somebody wants to go deeper,

1401
00:57:47.418 --> 00:57:50.526
the resources are there to do that.

1402
00:57:50.526 --> 00:57:51.937
a And that was always our thing.

1403
00:57:51.937 --> 00:57:53.700
There are teachers out there

1404
00:57:53.700 --> 00:57:58.170
who are far more qualified and experienced than I am.

1405
00:57:58.170 --> 00:58:00.210
And there are so many different places

1406
00:58:00.210 --> 00:58:02.220
you can go and learn about this stuff.

1407
00:58:02.220 --> 00:58:06.240
But they will only ever attract a certain number of people.

1408
00:58:06.240 --> 00:58:07.290
And we're probably talking

1409
00:58:07.290 --> 00:58:09.600
less than 5% of the world's population.

1410
00:58:09.600 --> 00:58:11.850
So what about the other 95%

1411
00:58:11.850 --> 00:58:14.850
who have never even heard of meditation

1412
00:58:14.850 --> 00:58:16.560
or looked at it or have thought about it,

1413
00:58:16.560 --> 00:58:18.810
and thought it wasn't for them or tried it,

1414
00:58:18.810 --> 00:58:20.730
and felt they couldn't do it.

1415
00:58:20.730 --> 00:58:22.530
Or automatically turned off to it.

1416
00:58:22.530 --> 00:58:24.960
Exactly.
Because of sort of reasons

1417
00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:26.743
that have nothing to do with the value of it,

1418
00:58:26.743 --> 00:58:29.040
because of what somebody's wearing or something.

1419
00:58:29.040 --> 00:58:30.896
Exactly. And that's what excites me.

1420
00:58:30.896 --> 00:58:33.840
Putting meditation in front of people

1421
00:58:33.840 --> 00:58:36.330
who've never even thought about it or putting it in places

1422
00:58:36.330 --> 00:58:38.100
where it's never existed.

1423
00:58:38.100 --> 00:58:41.190
That's I think what kind of drives the passion.

1424
00:58:41.190 --> 00:58:42.150
Well, let's talk about

1425
00:58:42.150 --> 00:58:43.980
the science and the benefits.

1426
00:58:43.980 --> 00:58:45.690
Let's talk about neuroplasticity.

1427
00:58:45.690 --> 00:58:48.810
Let's talk about focus, all of these things.

1428
00:58:48.810 --> 00:58:52.492
Let's assume that I'm a listener, I'm listening to this,

1429
00:58:52.492 --> 00:58:55.650
and this is my first introduction to meditate.

1430
00:58:55.650 --> 00:58:57.330
I mean, of course I've heard of meditation,

1431
00:58:57.330 --> 00:58:59.790
but I'm not convinced that this is something

1432
00:58:59.790 --> 00:59:01.980
that I really need to spend any time on.

1433
00:59:01.980 --> 00:59:02.813
Yeah.

1434
00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:07.350
So, number one, I would say,

1435
00:59:07.350 --> 00:59:10.500
I never tell anyone they should meditate.

1436
00:59:10.500 --> 00:59:13.440
I would just say from a scientific point of view, look.

1437
00:59:13.440 --> 00:59:15.330
There are these research studies that have been done.

1438
00:59:15.330 --> 00:59:17.340
And in these research studies,

1439
00:59:17.340 --> 00:59:20.220
there have been many, many benefits

1440
00:59:20.220 --> 00:59:21.520
that have been discovered.

1441
00:59:22.926 --> 00:59:26.130
I would recommend that you have a look at those.

1442
00:59:26.130 --> 00:59:28.740
If you're inspired to try, give it a go.

1443
00:59:28.740 --> 00:59:30.960
Base it on, don't do it because someone else

1444
00:59:30.960 --> 00:59:32.280
tells you to do it.

1445
00:59:32.280 --> 00:59:35.700
Do it because you feel motivated to do it,

1446
00:59:35.700 --> 00:59:37.020
and then continue to do it

1447
00:59:37.020 --> 00:59:40.320
because you recognize the benefit yourself.

1448
00:59:40.320 --> 00:59:42.210
In terms of the medicine, internally,

1449
00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:44.670
they're not obviously with us in the recording studio today.

1450
00:59:44.670 --> 00:59:47.490
I would always defer. We have a chief medical officer.

1451
00:59:47.490 --> 00:59:50.910
Dr. David Cox, and we also have a neuroscientist, Claudia.

1452
00:59:50.910 --> 00:59:54.360
And both of them are far more adept

1453
00:59:54.360 --> 00:59:55.380
in talking about this stuff.

1454
00:59:55.380 --> 00:59:59.250
But as you mentioned earlier, there are over 5,000 papers.

1455
00:59:59.250 --> 01:00:01.680
Peer reviewed, published studies.

1456
01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:04.650
Showing that meditation mindfulness can help us

1457
01:00:04.650 --> 01:00:07.822
with everything from reducing anxiety,

1458
01:00:07.822 --> 01:00:11.850
reducing depression and the relapse of depression.

1459
01:00:11.850 --> 01:00:14.100
Decreasing the incidence of insomnia,

1460
01:00:14.100 --> 01:00:17.313
improving heart health, decreasing our cholesterol levels.

1461
01:00:18.495 --> 01:00:21.027
All the way through to,

1462
01:00:21.027 --> 01:00:22.800
I mean, there's sort of stuff around chronic pain.

1463
01:00:22.800 --> 01:00:26.430
There's increasing levels of empathy.

1464
01:00:26.430 --> 01:00:29.164
And sometimes you look at the spread of this,

1465
01:00:29.164 --> 01:00:31.110
and it's like, how is this even possible?

1466
01:00:31.110 --> 01:00:34.920
How can one thing have this impact

1467
01:00:34.920 --> 01:00:36.507
on so many different things?

1468
01:00:36.507 --> 01:00:39.570
And I think we just underestimate the power of the mind.

1469
01:00:39.570 --> 01:00:41.340
The body and mind aren't separate.

1470
01:00:41.340 --> 01:00:44.520
We know when we get stressed in the mind that we feel it.

1471
01:00:44.520 --> 01:00:46.650
We experience it in the body.

1472
01:00:46.650 --> 01:00:48.540
We know when we are really relaxed and happy,

1473
01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:50.850
we feel that in the body.

1474
01:00:50.850 --> 01:00:53.970
So it's maybe not such a surprise

1475
01:00:53.970 --> 01:00:56.490
that we see these kind of benefits arise.

1476
01:00:56.490 --> 01:00:58.740
Well, when we look at the amount of time, energy,

1477
01:00:58.740 --> 01:01:02.040
and money spent on sort of taking care of other things

1478
01:01:02.040 --> 01:01:03.273
that are less important.

1479
01:01:04.170 --> 01:01:07.380
Whether it's shampoo for your hair, or brushing your teeth.

1480
01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:08.213
Speak for yourself.

1481
01:01:08.213 --> 01:01:10.628
Yeah, right, right.

1482
01:01:10.628 --> 01:01:13.080
What do we do to tend to our mental health?

1483
01:01:13.080 --> 01:01:15.900
Yeah, well we, oh, I guess, when we get home,

1484
01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:17.610
we pour ourselves a cocktail

1485
01:01:17.610 --> 01:01:19.320
and we watch "Dancing with the Stars",

1486
01:01:19.320 --> 01:01:21.540
and that's our way of relaxing.

1487
01:01:21.540 --> 01:01:23.250
Or watch a football game or something like that.

1488
01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:25.890
And that's not tending to,

1489
01:01:25.890 --> 01:01:29.130
that's not doing sort of mental pushups.

1490
01:01:29.130 --> 01:01:30.147
Not at all.

1491
01:01:30.147 --> 01:01:32.040
And there's different ways.

1492
01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:35.130
Some people, it's interesting, when I look at,

1493
01:01:35.130 --> 01:01:38.400
we work with professional sports people and sports teams,

1494
01:01:38.400 --> 01:01:39.720
and corporations, and for them,

1495
01:01:39.720 --> 01:01:42.513
it's more about focus, productivity.

1496
01:01:43.415 --> 01:01:46.143
And for them, it is about, it's like training the mind.

1497
01:01:46.143 --> 01:01:48.120
Like you say it's almost mental pushups.

1498
01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:49.440
For a very specific purpose.

1499
01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:50.970
Exactly.
Right.

1500
01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:52.080
And then I think for,

1501
01:01:52.080 --> 01:01:53.970
I actually believe for a bigger demographic,

1502
01:01:53.970 --> 01:01:56.910
it's more like, how can I sleep better at night?

1503
01:01:56.910 --> 01:01:59.190
How can I feel a little less stressed?

1504
01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:02.370
How can I have better relationships with those around me?

1505
01:02:02.370 --> 01:02:05.370
And those things, they're huge.

1506
01:02:05.370 --> 01:02:08.460
Make even a small shift in one of those areas in life,

1507
01:02:08.460 --> 01:02:11.670
and that is a life that's transformed right away.

1508
01:02:11.670 --> 01:02:14.280
So I take it that, although your doctor,

1509
01:02:14.280 --> 01:02:17.910
before you left on your adventure to become a monk

1510
01:02:17.910 --> 01:02:20.160
had suggested that you take Prozac,

1511
01:02:20.160 --> 01:02:23.580
that Prozac at some point did not become necessary.

1512
01:02:23.580 --> 01:02:25.298
I never took Prozac.

1513
01:02:25.298 --> 01:02:28.260
You seem like an affable, happy guy.

1514
01:02:28.260 --> 01:02:31.653
Yeah. I never went down that route.

1515
01:02:32.505 --> 01:02:34.680
Well, it's very tempting sometimes,

1516
01:02:34.680 --> 01:02:36.510
when a doctor or a person of authority

1517
01:02:36.510 --> 01:02:37.740
tells us to do something,

1518
01:02:37.740 --> 01:02:39.840
it can be tempting to go down there.

1519
01:02:39.840 --> 01:02:41.940
I mean, when you look at, at sort of

1520
01:02:41.940 --> 01:02:46.050
the state of mental health and the extent to which we,

1521
01:02:46.050 --> 01:02:49.256
we're so quick to medicate people

1522
01:02:49.256 --> 01:02:53.370
to deal with their sort of happiness issues,

1523
01:02:53.370 --> 01:02:54.660
depression, et cetera.

1524
01:02:54.660 --> 01:02:56.043
What do you make of that?

1525
01:02:57.147 --> 01:02:59.670
I'm definitely not anti-medication.

1526
01:02:59.670 --> 01:03:04.670
I feel that there are times when it's extremely important,

1527
01:03:04.890 --> 01:03:05.970
and very, very helpful.

1528
01:03:05.970 --> 01:03:09.150
So I don't think it's meditation versus medication.

1529
01:03:09.150 --> 01:03:12.794
But I do think we have a tendency,

1530
01:03:12.794 --> 01:03:16.110
and I would say this is especially true in the US,

1531
01:03:16.110 --> 01:03:18.510
having lived in a lot of different countries,

1532
01:03:18.510 --> 01:03:22.590
to over medicate, and to medicate a little too quickly.

1533
01:03:22.590 --> 01:03:25.260
Someone joked to me when I said I was moving to LA,

1534
01:03:25.260 --> 01:03:26.400
and they said, wow, over there,

1535
01:03:26.400 --> 01:03:28.560
you can hear people rattle as they walk down the street,

1536
01:03:28.560 --> 01:03:32.940
the pills in their pockets kind of shaking around.

1537
01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:37.940
And I am constantly astonished not only how many people

1538
01:03:38.850 --> 01:03:43.850
self medicate, but also how openly people talk about it.

1539
01:03:43.859 --> 01:03:45.750
There's no stigma.

1540
01:03:45.750 --> 01:03:47.280
There's no stigma whatsoever,

1541
01:03:47.280 --> 01:03:50.160
and there is in some countries I would say.

1542
01:03:50.160 --> 01:03:53.580
So I would love to see a shift.

1543
01:03:53.580 --> 01:03:56.670
I'd love to see a shift in terms of prevention.

1544
01:03:56.670 --> 01:03:59.321
So I think far too often in our society,

1545
01:03:59.321 --> 01:04:01.680
we wait until something happens

1546
01:04:01.680 --> 01:04:03.660
until trying to kind of fix it.

1547
01:04:03.660 --> 01:04:06.510
And that's what the medication kind of thing is.

1548
01:04:06.510 --> 01:04:08.280
There's already a problem.

1549
01:04:08.280 --> 01:04:10.710
If we can get into a pattern in society,

1550
01:04:10.710 --> 01:04:13.020
whether it's ourselves, the next generation,

1551
01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:15.180
preferably both, where we're taking

1552
01:04:15.180 --> 01:04:17.010
preventative care of the mind,

1553
01:04:17.010 --> 01:04:19.791
where we're actually carving out, prioritizing,

1554
01:04:19.791 --> 01:04:22.860
whether it's 10, 15 minutes in a day, wherever it might be,

1555
01:04:22.860 --> 01:04:25.860
to look after, to clean the mind each day.

1556
01:04:25.860 --> 01:04:27.690
We don't even need to get to the point

1557
01:04:27.690 --> 01:04:29.940
where we have to decide whether to medicate or not.

1558
01:04:29.940 --> 01:04:33.060
That's I think the potential.

1559
01:04:33.060 --> 01:04:34.080
That's beautifully put.

1560
01:04:34.080 --> 01:04:37.269
And to me, that's the future of medicine.

1561
01:04:37.269 --> 01:04:39.946
Functional medicine, preventive medicine.

1562
01:04:39.946 --> 01:04:43.080
In the physical realm as well.

1563
01:04:43.080 --> 01:04:44.640
And it's no coincidence.

1564
01:04:44.640 --> 01:04:48.690
We have 54 research studies on the table right now,

1565
01:04:48.690 --> 01:04:50.670
34 are in motion.

1566
01:04:50.670 --> 01:04:52.590
And these are all reverse engineered.

1567
01:04:52.590 --> 01:04:57.000
So these are hospitals, clinics, universities,

1568
01:04:57.000 --> 01:04:59.340
coming to us and saying,

1569
01:04:59.340 --> 01:05:04.038
we believe that mindfulness and meditation

1570
01:05:04.038 --> 01:05:06.900
can be a meaningful intervention

1571
01:05:06.900 --> 01:05:08.700
with these types of symptoms.

1572
01:05:08.700 --> 01:05:10.230
Can we test it?

1573
01:05:10.230 --> 01:05:12.990
And we are seeing time and time again,

1574
01:05:12.990 --> 01:05:14.190
it makes a difference.

1575
01:05:14.190 --> 01:05:17.640
And the more the medical community embrace that,

1576
01:05:17.640 --> 01:05:20.460
the more I think we'll see it move from, right now,

1577
01:05:20.460 --> 01:05:22.560
it's being seen more of a treatment.

1578
01:05:22.560 --> 01:05:23.820
Management, treatment.

1579
01:05:23.820 --> 01:05:25.710
But I think over time, it'll move from treatment

1580
01:05:25.710 --> 01:05:27.483
to management to prevention.

1581
01:05:29.190 --> 01:05:31.770
We're talking about the health impacts,

1582
01:05:31.770 --> 01:05:34.890
we're talking about the improved ability to focus,

1583
01:05:34.890 --> 01:05:36.900
we're talking about the impact on happiness.

1584
01:05:36.900 --> 01:05:39.660
But for me, in my personal experience,

1585
01:05:39.660 --> 01:05:44.660
the most profound impact that meditation has had on me is,

1586
01:05:46.560 --> 01:05:48.660
well, first, sort of the watershed moment

1587
01:05:48.660 --> 01:05:51.030
of truly sort of beginning to understand

1588
01:05:51.030 --> 01:05:53.160
that there's a difference between who I am

1589
01:05:53.160 --> 01:05:55.200
and what my brain is saying.

1590
01:05:55.200 --> 01:05:57.060
The mental chatter of my mind.

1591
01:05:57.060 --> 01:06:01.950
And then kind of tangentially to that, this idea of story.

1592
01:06:01.950 --> 01:06:04.560
You talked about the power of story, your story,

1593
01:06:04.560 --> 01:06:06.420
how everybody has a story.

1594
01:06:06.420 --> 01:06:10.560
And we all tell ourselves a story about ourselves.

1595
01:06:10.560 --> 01:06:12.810
And generally, unless you're an incredibly

1596
01:06:12.810 --> 01:06:15.660
well adjusted person, you're usually not telling

1597
01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:17.970
the greatest story about yourself.

1598
01:06:17.970 --> 01:06:19.920
And you're hanging your hat on certain things

1599
01:06:19.920 --> 01:06:22.074
that may have happened to you when you were younger.

1600
01:06:22.074 --> 01:06:22.907
Yeah.

1601
01:06:22.907 --> 01:06:24.090
And you use those as reasons

1602
01:06:24.090 --> 01:06:25.268
to do or not do things.

1603
01:06:25.268 --> 01:06:26.101
Yeah.

1604
01:06:26.101 --> 01:06:29.310
And what meditation has given me is, first,

1605
01:06:29.310 --> 01:06:31.710
just a greater awareness of that simple fact,

1606
01:06:31.710 --> 01:06:35.370
and then tools and the sort of acuity and ability

1607
01:06:35.370 --> 01:06:38.047
to reframe that story, to tell a new story.

1608
01:06:38.047 --> 01:06:38.910
Yeah.

1609
01:06:38.910 --> 01:06:41.220
To empower me to understand

1610
01:06:41.220 --> 01:06:42.652
that I have control over that.

1611
01:06:42.652 --> 01:06:43.675
Yeah.

1612
01:06:43.675 --> 01:06:45.720
And I don't have to be a passive,

1613
01:06:45.720 --> 01:06:47.970
I don't have to be a passenger on that bus

1614
01:06:47.970 --> 01:06:50.100
where this is just,

1615
01:06:50.100 --> 01:06:52.560
I'm assuming that this is just what it is.

1616
01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:53.970
Yeah, that's a beautiful way

1617
01:06:53.970 --> 01:06:56.400
of describing it, I think.

1618
01:06:56.400 --> 01:07:00.720
So often words like freedom and liberation

1619
01:07:00.720 --> 01:07:02.324
are used around meditation.

1620
01:07:02.324 --> 01:07:05.370
Well, sometimes it can sound a bit kind of much

1621
01:07:05.370 --> 01:07:07.560
to sort of liberation, but there is.

1622
01:07:07.560 --> 01:07:10.800
It's a sense of being liberated. We let go.

1623
01:07:10.800 --> 01:07:12.810
We put down our baggage from the past.

1624
01:07:12.810 --> 01:07:15.270
We let go of our expectations for the future.

1625
01:07:15.270 --> 01:07:17.490
And in that moment we're free.

1626
01:07:17.490 --> 01:07:19.367
There is freedom.

1627
01:07:19.367 --> 01:07:23.190
And when we are not free, it's almost self-imposed,

1628
01:07:23.190 --> 01:07:25.880
but we don't realize what it is we're doing

1629
01:07:25.880 --> 01:07:28.140
that is causing this sense of being constrained,

1630
01:07:28.140 --> 01:07:30.600
and then when we discover that sense of freedom,

1631
01:07:30.600 --> 01:07:34.407
I just think, yeah, life has changed.

1632
01:07:34.407 --> 01:07:37.788
Speaking of life changing, you're a father now.

1633
01:07:37.788 --> 01:07:38.621
I am.

1634
01:07:38.621 --> 01:07:39.730
I am.
So how does this

1635
01:07:40.680 --> 01:07:45.680
color your experience as meditator, as a business person?

1636
01:07:46.410 --> 01:07:47.910
Well, I mean, compared to you, Rich,

1637
01:07:47.910 --> 01:07:50.850
I'm a very, very inexperienced father.

1638
01:07:50.850 --> 01:07:54.060
Yes, but you're in the young years

1639
01:07:54.060 --> 01:07:57.600
where it's most, you know, it's pretty intense.

1640
01:07:57.600 --> 01:07:58.698
It is.

1641
01:07:58.698 --> 01:08:02.550
And I think we're almost six months in.

1642
01:08:02.550 --> 01:08:03.383
Wow.

1643
01:08:03.383 --> 01:08:06.060
So we have a baby boy called Harley.

1644
01:08:06.060 --> 01:08:08.493
And he's a joy.

1645
01:08:09.870 --> 01:08:14.640
Like every new parent will say, I'm sure it is both a joy,

1646
01:08:14.640 --> 01:08:16.950
but also incredibly hard work.

1647
01:08:16.950 --> 01:08:18.420
And definitely, look.

1648
01:08:18.420 --> 01:08:23.420
Juggling Headspace and Harley is tricky,

1649
01:08:24.660 --> 01:08:27.810
as it is for any kind of, I was gonna say working parent,

1650
01:08:27.810 --> 01:08:29.910
doesn't matter whether you're working or not.

1651
01:08:29.910 --> 01:08:31.260
Looking after a baby, a kid,

1652
01:08:31.260 --> 01:08:33.738
that's a full-time job right there.

1653
01:08:33.738 --> 01:08:37.620
And I think the demands of Headspace insomuch

1654
01:08:37.620 --> 01:08:42.033
as kind of traveling a lot make it quite quite difficult.

1655
01:08:44.370 --> 01:08:45.570
But at the same time,

1656
01:08:45.570 --> 01:08:47.940
it just makes me really kind of cherish the time

1657
01:08:47.940 --> 01:08:51.570
when I am at home with my wife and Harley,

1658
01:08:51.570 --> 01:08:53.340
just makes me enjoy it that much more.

1659
01:08:53.340 --> 01:08:56.250
Have you found that you need to

1660
01:08:56.250 --> 01:08:58.380
increase your meditation game?

1661
01:08:58.380 --> 01:09:00.960
Or have you found that now you understand

1662
01:09:00.960 --> 01:09:03.270
why people give you the excuse of...

1663
01:09:03.270 --> 01:09:04.410
I don't have any time.

1664
01:09:04.410 --> 01:09:05.243
Yes.

1665
01:09:05.243 --> 01:09:06.420
Yeah. I do.

1666
01:09:06.420 --> 01:09:09.600
I have a much better understanding.

1667
01:09:09.600 --> 01:09:14.600
I still believe, and at the moment, I mean, I only have one.

1668
01:09:15.247 --> 01:09:18.570
So at the moment I am able to find the time.

1669
01:09:18.570 --> 01:09:20.220
And I carve out the time.

1670
01:09:20.220 --> 01:09:22.470
What is your routine now?

1671
01:09:22.470 --> 01:09:27.470
Well, before Harley was born, I would get up very early.

1672
01:09:27.540 --> 01:09:28.890
I would do my meditation, usually around-

1673
01:09:28.890 --> 01:09:30.270
Very early is a sliding,

1674
01:09:30.270 --> 01:09:31.986
is a very relative term with you.

1675
01:09:31.986 --> 01:09:33.330
(Andy laughing)

1676
01:09:33.330 --> 01:09:35.141
Are we talking 2:30?

1677
01:09:35.141 --> 01:09:36.600
Not that early, no.

1678
01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:38.220
Shocking, I suppose it's not that early.

1679
01:09:38.220 --> 01:09:40.050
I'd probably get up at like 5:00.

1680
01:09:40.050 --> 01:09:41.580
I'd meditate for about an hour,

1681
01:09:41.580 --> 01:09:44.130
and then I'd go surfing with Richie,

1682
01:09:44.130 --> 01:09:46.650
and then we'd have some breakfast and come into work.

1683
01:09:46.650 --> 01:09:48.651
Where do you like to surf?

1684
01:09:48.651 --> 01:09:51.949
If it's a working morning, so during the week,

1685
01:09:51.949 --> 01:09:53.460
we just go local.

1686
01:09:53.460 --> 01:09:57.184
So OP, or Venice, somewhere like that.

1687
01:09:57.184 --> 01:09:59.550
But if it's the weekend, we'll go a bit further,

1688
01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:01.950
further afield north or south.

1689
01:10:01.950 --> 01:10:04.353
But obviously now, that's changed a little bit.

1690
01:10:05.250 --> 01:10:07.200
My wife is up during the night.

1691
01:10:07.200 --> 01:10:09.996
And so my kind of shift, if you like,

1692
01:10:09.996 --> 01:10:12.788
is the morning shift, 'cause I'm up early anyway.

1693
01:10:12.788 --> 01:10:16.830
I do that while she kind of gets some more sleep.

1694
01:10:16.830 --> 01:10:18.600
So I tend to spend that time now

1695
01:10:18.600 --> 01:10:21.270
between sort of 5:00 and 7:00 in the morning

1696
01:10:21.270 --> 01:10:25.320
looking after Harley, we hang out a bit, we play a bit.

1697
01:10:25.320 --> 01:10:27.480
Sometimes I'll do a bit of meditation.

1698
01:10:27.480 --> 01:10:30.540
If he's settled, and quite kind of content,

1699
01:10:30.540 --> 01:10:33.480
then I'll do a bit of meditation,

1700
01:10:33.480 --> 01:10:35.518
and he just kind of hangs out.

1701
01:10:35.518 --> 01:10:36.840
Very cool.

1702
01:10:36.840 --> 01:10:39.341
Well, Andy, listen.

1703
01:10:39.341 --> 01:10:41.824
I tried this meditation thing.

1704
01:10:41.824 --> 01:10:43.740
I can't get my brain to shut off.

1705
01:10:43.740 --> 01:10:45.813
It just doesn't work for me.

1706
01:10:47.430 --> 01:10:48.263
Really?

1707
01:10:48.263 --> 01:10:50.067
No, I'm saying,

1708
01:10:50.067 --> 01:10:51.990
I'm not talking about me, I'm just saying,

1709
01:10:51.990 --> 01:10:54.187
you must have people say that to you all the time.

1710
01:10:54.187 --> 01:10:55.950
How do you respond

1711
01:10:55.950 --> 01:10:58.723
to that lament?
Well, I get very excited.

1712
01:10:58.723 --> 01:11:00.120
I get very excited about it.

1713
01:11:00.120 --> 01:11:04.050
Because I know there is an opportunity

1714
01:11:04.050 --> 01:11:05.340
for transformation right there.

1715
01:11:05.340 --> 01:11:08.730
Because there is nobody who cannot meditate.

1716
01:11:08.730 --> 01:11:13.730
And what excites me about it is that person hasn't yet found

1717
01:11:13.980 --> 01:11:17.370
or hasn't yet been shown the way to approach it

1718
01:11:17.370 --> 01:11:19.885
in such a way that they get to experience

1719
01:11:19.885 --> 01:11:22.170
what they think it might be.

1720
01:11:22.170 --> 01:11:27.170
And so it's helping them find that approach.

1721
01:11:29.310 --> 01:11:32.790
'Cause thoughts are thoughts.

1722
01:11:32.790 --> 01:11:36.090
That underlying sense of stillness and calm

1723
01:11:36.090 --> 01:11:37.170
is always with us.

1724
01:11:37.170 --> 01:11:39.720
It's not unique to one individual.

1725
01:11:39.720 --> 01:11:40.950
Some people find it easier

1726
01:11:40.950 --> 01:11:43.740
to let go of thoughts and experience that calm.

1727
01:11:43.740 --> 01:11:46.290
But everybody has the potential to do it.

1728
01:11:46.290 --> 01:11:50.160
Some people experience it the moment they first sit down.

1729
01:11:50.160 --> 01:11:51.810
For others, it can take a number of days,

1730
01:11:51.810 --> 01:11:54.270
for others it can take a number of weeks.

1731
01:11:54.270 --> 01:11:56.640
But helping people understand

1732
01:11:56.640 --> 01:12:00.120
that it's not about switching off thoughts.

1733
01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:02.460
It's not about clearing the mind.

1734
01:12:02.460 --> 01:12:04.590
If that's the way you approach meditation,

1735
01:12:04.590 --> 01:12:07.375
then of course you're gonna feel terrible about it,

1736
01:12:07.375 --> 01:12:10.512
because it is the nature of the mind to think.

1737
01:12:10.512 --> 01:12:13.680
So if we can kind of flip that on its head and say, okay.

1738
01:12:13.680 --> 01:12:16.230
Instead, meditation is actually about,

1739
01:12:16.230 --> 01:12:19.350
how can we learn to step back out of the traffic,

1740
01:12:19.350 --> 01:12:22.143
out of the stream of thought, and simply be present?

1741
01:12:23.220 --> 01:12:24.300
Then it doesn't matter, actually.

1742
01:12:24.300 --> 01:12:26.640
if we can really feel confident and comfortable with that,

1743
01:12:26.640 --> 01:12:29.370
doesn't matter if the mind is busy, whether it's quiet.

1744
01:12:29.370 --> 01:12:32.160
So you can have the busiest mind in the world,

1745
01:12:32.160 --> 01:12:34.260
and you're okay with it.

1746
01:12:34.260 --> 01:12:37.170
My friend, Charlie, do you know Charlie Knowles?

1747
01:12:37.170 --> 01:12:38.250
Meditation teacher?
I know the name,

1748
01:12:38.250 --> 01:12:39.083
yeah, I know the name.

1749
01:12:39.083 --> 01:12:40.770
Do you know Tom Knowles?

1750
01:12:40.770 --> 01:12:43.320
He's a well known Vedic.

1751
01:12:43.320 --> 01:12:44.153
Familiar.

1752
01:12:44.153 --> 01:12:45.750
Yeah, Charlie's his son,

1753
01:12:45.750 --> 01:12:48.270
and Charlie does a lot of stuff around Venice.

1754
01:12:48.270 --> 01:12:50.790
He started this thing called The Path that they're doing.

1755
01:12:50.790 --> 01:12:52.410
Yes, I have heard about that.

1756
01:12:52.410 --> 01:12:53.880
With Dina, is that with...

1757
01:12:53.880 --> 01:12:55.830
Yeah, I don't know her, but yeah.

1758
01:12:55.830 --> 01:12:56.663
He has a partner.
Yeah, I think so,

1759
01:12:56.663 --> 01:12:57.971
that's right.
Right, right.

1760
01:12:57.971 --> 01:12:58.960
So they have it in New York.

1761
01:12:58.960 --> 01:12:59.793
Yes, that's right.

1762
01:12:59.793 --> 01:13:01.560
And Charlie famously says

1763
01:13:01.560 --> 01:13:04.140
that meditation is failure proof.

1764
01:13:04.140 --> 01:13:05.820
You cannot fail at meditation.

1765
01:13:05.820 --> 01:13:08.400
Simply by virtue of the fact that you're doing it,

1766
01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:09.720
you are succeeding at it.

1767
01:13:09.720 --> 01:13:11.400
Because when that thought enters,

1768
01:13:11.400 --> 01:13:13.890
and then you develop an awareness that you're having that,

1769
01:13:13.890 --> 01:13:15.120
you can always bring it back.

1770
01:13:15.120 --> 01:13:16.740
And that is the practice.

1771
01:13:16.740 --> 01:13:18.090
Yeah.

1772
01:13:18.090 --> 01:13:19.320
Right? Is that fair?

1773
01:13:19.320 --> 01:13:20.674
Yeah, absolutely.

1774
01:13:20.674 --> 01:13:22.500
My teacher kind of used to sum it up like this.

1775
01:13:22.500 --> 01:13:23.333
He used to say,

1776
01:13:23.333 --> 01:13:26.430
there is no such thing as good or bad meditation.

1777
01:13:26.430 --> 01:13:29.511
There is only awareness, or non awareness.

1778
01:13:29.511 --> 01:13:31.830
And that's it. You can't fail.

1779
01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:33.060
I love that.

1780
01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:35.370
I think that's a good place to wrap it up.

1781
01:13:35.370 --> 01:13:36.203
Sounds good.

1782
01:13:36.203 --> 01:13:38.790
But I do want to know what's in store,

1783
01:13:38.790 --> 01:13:42.360
if you're willing to share for the future,

1784
01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:44.340
for version three.

1785
01:13:44.340 --> 01:13:45.540
Yeah.
Headspace,

1786
01:13:45.540 --> 01:13:49.822
where are you taking this global domination of meditation?

1787
01:13:49.822 --> 01:13:51.840
(Andy laughing)

1788
01:13:51.840 --> 01:13:55.290
Well, in a very physical, tangible sense,

1789
01:13:55.290 --> 01:13:58.950
We are taking it to English speaking territory.

1790
01:13:58.950 --> 01:14:01.680
So very excited, we're heading off to Canada

1791
01:14:01.680 --> 01:14:05.943
this coming week, then we're off to Hong Kong and Singapore.

1792
01:14:07.020 --> 01:14:08.883
Scandinavia, Holland.

1793
01:14:08.883 --> 01:14:10.620
So lots of countries around the world

1794
01:14:10.620 --> 01:14:14.580
where English language is widely spoken.

1795
01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:16.440
In terms of the development of V3,

1796
01:14:16.440 --> 01:14:19.650
we're gonna do it in exactly the same way as we did V2.

1797
01:14:19.650 --> 01:14:24.330
So V1, we built, and then we just listened.

1798
01:14:24.330 --> 01:14:26.400
And we listened to feedback.

1799
01:14:26.400 --> 01:14:29.670
And we listened to what the user wanted.

1800
01:14:29.670 --> 01:14:34.140
And we put that together in a plan, and we built V2.

1801
01:14:34.140 --> 01:14:36.107
And when we built V2, kind of,

1802
01:14:36.107 --> 01:14:38.070
we are a lot further along the line now,

1803
01:14:38.070 --> 01:14:40.980
we're closer to what we originally intended,

1804
01:14:40.980 --> 01:14:42.630
what we wanted to build.

1805
01:14:42.630 --> 01:14:43.920
We are not there yet.

1806
01:14:43.920 --> 01:14:46.975
I think there are certain elements that are very obvious,

1807
01:14:46.975 --> 01:14:49.320
the community is one.

1808
01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:51.390
I don't think we've even begun with the community.

1809
01:14:51.390 --> 01:14:53.940
At the moment it's the buddy system where you can have

1810
01:14:53.940 --> 01:14:56.520
up to kind of five friends or family, it's like a gym buddy,

1811
01:14:56.520 --> 01:14:59.910
and you can check in on them, they can check in on you.

1812
01:14:59.910 --> 01:15:02.310
I think we'd like to develop this sense of community.

1813
01:15:02.310 --> 01:15:03.870
So traditionally, meditation,

1814
01:15:03.870 --> 01:15:05.670
there was always a teacher,

1815
01:15:05.670 --> 01:15:07.890
and there was a set of techniques,

1816
01:15:07.890 --> 01:15:09.090
and then there was a community,

1817
01:15:09.090 --> 01:15:10.710
and those three things sit together,

1818
01:15:10.710 --> 01:15:14.100
kind of supporting one another, like three legs of a stool.

1819
01:15:14.100 --> 01:15:17.119
So we're really keen to kind of make the community

1820
01:15:17.119 --> 01:15:18.990
a bigger part of it.

1821
01:15:18.990 --> 01:15:22.260
But yeah, most of all, we'll listen to the community,

1822
01:15:22.260 --> 01:15:23.490
even in terms of the packs.

1823
01:15:23.490 --> 01:15:26.430
Right now, it's very democratic.

1824
01:15:26.430 --> 01:15:29.313
We have a list of suggested packs.

1825
01:15:30.420 --> 01:15:32.850
Once a month it goes out to the community.

1826
01:15:32.850 --> 01:15:34.440
They get to vote on it.

1827
01:15:34.440 --> 01:15:36.510
And whatever they vote on,

1828
01:15:36.510 --> 01:15:38.760
that gets recorded as the next pack.

1829
01:15:38.760 --> 01:15:39.720
Interesting.

1830
01:15:39.720 --> 01:15:41.910
There's so many cool things happening

1831
01:15:41.910 --> 01:15:44.460
in the kind of health space, in the app health space

1832
01:15:44.460 --> 01:15:47.010
with these apps getting smarter and smarter

1833
01:15:47.010 --> 01:15:48.990
about reading your behavior patterns.

1834
01:15:48.990 --> 01:15:52.200
and kind of what you do and anticipating.

1835
01:15:52.200 --> 01:15:54.150
There's gotta be a way to kind of build that in

1836
01:15:54.150 --> 01:15:56.790
to sort of have, whether it's something as basic

1837
01:15:56.790 --> 01:15:59.820
as reminders, but also, hey,

1838
01:15:59.820 --> 01:16:00.870
here's the part of the day

1839
01:16:00.870 --> 01:16:02.850
when your heart rate starts to go up,

1840
01:16:02.850 --> 01:16:04.043
or these kinds of things.
Yeah.

1841
01:16:04.043 --> 01:16:07.110
There's definitely stuff like calendar integration

1842
01:16:07.110 --> 01:16:07.943
and all that sort of thing

1843
01:16:07.943 --> 01:16:09.930
that we'll have in there for sure.

1844
01:16:09.930 --> 01:16:12.748
And together with kind of reminders and things.

1845
01:16:12.748 --> 01:16:15.870
In fact, there's even, it's fairly rudimentary,

1846
01:16:15.870 --> 01:16:18.840
but there's a reminder sort of function in there now.

1847
01:16:18.840 --> 01:16:22.350
I think one interesting area is wearables.

1848
01:16:22.350 --> 01:16:24.289
And we've not really kind of talked about it.

1849
01:16:24.289 --> 01:16:26.700
I've really mixed feelings about it.

1850
01:16:26.700 --> 01:16:29.700
I think there is something interesting there

1851
01:16:29.700 --> 01:16:31.770
in that there is the potential, again, for,

1852
01:16:31.770 --> 01:16:34.020
it's a conversation to take place.

1853
01:16:34.020 --> 01:16:36.000
So let's just say it's a watch.

1854
01:16:36.000 --> 01:16:39.630
And the watch recognizes that your heart rate is spiking.

1855
01:16:39.630 --> 01:16:40.770
Okay.

1856
01:16:40.770 --> 01:16:42.930
It's kind of interesting, if your watch,

1857
01:16:42.930 --> 01:16:46.632
in recognizing that can then suggest to you and serve you up

1858
01:16:46.632 --> 01:16:50.100
a little exercise, a Headspace exercise to do.

1859
01:16:50.100 --> 01:16:54.060
That's a really nice conversation to be able to have.

1860
01:16:54.060 --> 01:16:56.260
It's a nice intervention to be able to have.

1861
01:16:57.360 --> 01:16:59.520
The tricky thing with wearables is,

1862
01:16:59.520 --> 01:17:03.330
meditation is not kind of goal orientated.

1863
01:17:03.330 --> 01:17:05.220
It's about kind of letting go.

1864
01:17:05.220 --> 01:17:06.870
And so it's a tricky balance

1865
01:17:06.870 --> 01:17:10.470
of not getting people too obsessed in the data.

1866
01:17:10.470 --> 01:17:13.020
So we're just playing around with that at the moment,

1867
01:17:13.020 --> 01:17:15.870
just trying to find the optimum kind of balance.

1868
01:17:15.870 --> 01:17:17.670
Yeah, that's very interesting.

1869
01:17:17.670 --> 01:17:18.510
Very interesting.

1870
01:17:18.510 --> 01:17:23.510
Because the typical Westerner needs that goal.

1871
01:17:23.610 --> 01:17:24.493
They love, yeah.
They're like, hey,

1872
01:17:24.493 --> 01:17:25.680
I gotta get through.

1873
01:17:25.680 --> 01:17:29.700
And the app is sort of orchestrated around catering to that

1874
01:17:29.700 --> 01:17:33.096
in the sense that like, hey, here's your first 10 days.

1875
01:17:33.096 --> 01:17:34.687
Get to get to 30 days.

1876
01:17:34.687 --> 01:17:35.520
Exactly.

1877
01:17:35.520 --> 01:17:36.630
And so...
Exactly.

1878
01:17:36.630 --> 01:17:38.825
There is some recognition of that.

1879
01:17:38.825 --> 01:17:43.080
We tried to do it in such a way that it's still,

1880
01:17:43.080 --> 01:17:46.380
it's sensitive of that need to want to kind of progress

1881
01:17:46.380 --> 01:17:47.880
and go on a journey.

1882
01:17:47.880 --> 01:17:49.500
And yet at the same time,

1883
01:17:49.500 --> 01:17:52.680
there's still a sense of sort of freedom and letting go,

1884
01:17:52.680 --> 01:17:56.250
and not trying to be too focused on the future,

1885
01:17:56.250 --> 01:17:58.350
instead being more sort of concerned

1886
01:17:58.350 --> 01:17:59.850
with the present moment.

1887
01:17:59.850 --> 01:18:00.683
Very cool.

1888
01:18:00.683 --> 01:18:03.330
Well, I'll be excited to see how that develops.

1889
01:18:03.330 --> 01:18:04.163
Thanks, man.

1890
01:18:04.163 --> 01:18:08.130
And maybe any final thoughts for the new meditator,

1891
01:18:08.130 --> 01:18:11.549
some words of encouragement, or maybe just a tip or two

1892
01:18:11.549 --> 01:18:13.838
that people could take away?
Sure.

1893
01:18:13.838 --> 01:18:17.100
Number one, it only works if you do it.

1894
01:18:17.100 --> 01:18:19.230
You can think about it all day long.

1895
01:18:19.230 --> 01:18:21.030
But unless you actually do it.

1896
01:18:21.030 --> 01:18:23.130
I met people and they've said,

1897
01:18:23.130 --> 01:18:24.477
oh, it's not really working for me.

1898
01:18:24.477 --> 01:18:26.310
And I said, how often do you do it?

1899
01:18:26.310 --> 01:18:28.710
Eh, well maybe sort of once a month or something.

1900
01:18:28.710 --> 01:18:31.080
Okay. Think about going to the gym once a month.

1901
01:18:31.080 --> 01:18:33.030
Are we really gonna see any kind of improvement?

1902
01:18:33.030 --> 01:18:34.380
Probably not.

1903
01:18:34.380 --> 01:18:36.393
So prioritizing it.

1904
01:18:37.320 --> 01:18:39.090
Unless you do it first thing in the morning,

1905
01:18:39.090 --> 01:18:40.710
there is a very good chance

1906
01:18:40.710 --> 01:18:43.020
that it's gonna slip down your to-do list.

1907
01:18:43.020 --> 01:18:47.910
And you're gonna find yourself in the evening, in bed,

1908
01:18:47.910 --> 01:18:51.660
tired, thinking, oh no, I haven't done my meditation again.

1909
01:18:51.660 --> 01:18:55.080
And even feeling stressed or guilty about this thing

1910
01:18:55.080 --> 01:18:58.050
that you started doing to feel less stressed about.

1911
01:18:58.050 --> 01:19:01.380
So do it first thing in the day.

1912
01:19:01.380 --> 01:19:02.880
Kind of almost attach it to something.

1913
01:19:02.880 --> 01:19:04.920
So it's like, okay, shower and meditate.

1914
01:19:04.920 --> 01:19:06.630
Or breakfast and meditate.

1915
01:19:06.630 --> 01:19:08.490
So it almost becomes part of your routine

1916
01:19:08.490 --> 01:19:10.080
in the same way as you brush your teeth

1917
01:19:10.080 --> 01:19:12.090
or whatever else you do in the morning.

1918
01:19:12.090 --> 01:19:14.040
But be flexible, if you can't do it in the morning,

1919
01:19:14.040 --> 01:19:16.650
find another time of the day.

1920
01:19:16.650 --> 01:19:18.324
Start off realistic.

1921
01:19:18.324 --> 01:19:19.923
You're a runner, Rich.

1922
01:19:20.940 --> 01:19:22.383
You'd never,

1923
01:19:23.370 --> 01:19:26.910
I'm sure have never have attempted a marathon

1924
01:19:26.910 --> 01:19:30.630
without starting at a slightly kind of

1925
01:19:30.630 --> 01:19:33.570
shorter sort of period, right?

1926
01:19:33.570 --> 01:19:34.673
Right, of course.

1927
01:19:34.673 --> 01:19:37.080
And for some reason, I dunno why it is,

1928
01:19:37.080 --> 01:19:39.060
but there's this idea that in order to get any benefit

1929
01:19:39.060 --> 01:19:41.220
out of meditation, we have do it a long time, right?

1930
01:19:41.220 --> 01:19:43.080
We have to sit there for an hour. We don't.

1931
01:19:43.080 --> 01:19:45.900
Science has shown us that the benefits are felt

1932
01:19:45.900 --> 01:19:48.020
and experienced within five minutes.

1933
01:19:48.020 --> 01:19:50.100
And now do you look back on your 19 hours a day,

1934
01:19:50.100 --> 01:19:52.752
going, what was that all about?

1935
01:19:52.752 --> 01:19:54.090
(Andy laughing)

1936
01:19:54.090 --> 01:19:55.860
Well, that's different.

1937
01:19:55.860 --> 01:19:59.271
I guess like anything, there's subtleties.

1938
01:19:59.271 --> 01:20:04.000
So be realistic. 10 minutes a day is fine.

1939
01:20:04.000 --> 01:20:06.867
Download the app. It's free to download.

1940
01:20:06.867 --> 01:20:09.000
And the Take 10, that 10 minute thing.

1941
01:20:09.000 --> 01:20:10.680
You can use that for the rest of your life.

1942
01:20:10.680 --> 01:20:13.923
We've had people use the free bit for two years.

1943
01:20:13.923 --> 01:20:17.220
And they never subscribe?
And they never subscribe.

1944
01:20:17.220 --> 01:20:20.850
And honestly, and I really mean this genuinely.

1945
01:20:20.850 --> 01:20:23.190
We put it out there so that people

1946
01:20:23.190 --> 01:20:26.160
who didn't want to subscribe or couldn't afford to subscribe

1947
01:20:26.160 --> 01:20:29.716
were still able to use it, to feel part of the community.

1948
01:20:29.716 --> 01:20:32.610
I'm happy if people use it in that way.

1949
01:20:32.610 --> 01:20:36.390
So download the app, try Take 10.

1950
01:20:36.390 --> 01:20:38.310
Prioritize it in your day and know

1951
01:20:38.310 --> 01:20:40.950
that it only works if you do it.

1952
01:20:40.950 --> 01:20:42.330
Right on.

